**Location:** “The unfalsifiability charge — heads I win, tails you lose — does not describe Summa’s object-level claims”. Ledger: “Lem’s forecasts are unfalsifiable by construction — ‘Heads I win, tails you lose’” → RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER. Lede: “one of them shows that half his case is wrong.”

**Problem:** Swirski’s “Heads I win” paragraph is his own parody (open-ended wonders for the year 1,002,022). He then turns to *Summa* as “a volume of futurological speculations par excellence” and lists *specific* anticipations he thinks landed (punctuated equilibrium, VR, search engines, nanobots, bionics, SETI’s failure). His case against reading Lem as a scientific futurologist is: fiction is underdetermined; Lem cherry-picks fiction; scientific futurology would be “an unequivocally formulated series of concrete predictions and reasonable timelines”; and (via the 1992 report) Lem’s real subject was conceptual limits. Scoring a joke-forecast method as a charge *against Summa’s object-level claims*, then declaring half the case refuted, is a straw man.

**Fix:** Grade the charge Swirski actually makes about *Summa*: that it is not scientific futurology because it does not publish dated, unequivocal predictions — or that hits are byproducts. If unfalsifiability stays, say it is his charge against the genre and against treating fiction as prophecy, not against the sentences this book scored.

---

### 5. Cherry-picking “fiction” is paid with a *Summa* endnote

**Location:** “he is not above cherry-picking his fiction for predictive hits” tied, in prose and in one ledger row, to the ariadnology self-grade. Verdict: RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON.

**Problem:** Swirski’s cherry-picking sentence (p. 38) is about *The Magellan Nebula* (trions / videoplastics dated to the 27th century; CDs in thirty years). The ariadnology claim is a separate paragraph about three later essay collections scoring *Summa*. One ledger row concatenates them. RAWR (“outcome matches; the mechanism he gave doesn’t”) does not fit a critic’s accusation. The real finding — later-Lem claimed the coinage as a search-engine hit while the same note forbids the method that worked — is a fact about Lem’s self-grade, not a confirmation of “cherry-picking his fiction.”

**Fix:** Split the row. Fiction-cherry-picking stays Swirski’s, unpaid here (those collections are not held). Ariadnology is a comparison of two scorecards of *Summa*; score that as the object-level collision and do not launder it through the fiction charge.

---

### 6. The two scorecards do not “collide once”

**Location:** “The two scorecards collide once”. Swirski “crediting Lem with… anticipating ‘artificial emotion, virtual reality, search engines, nanobots, bionics, and not least the failure of Frank Drake’s SETI’.”

**Problem:** Pitch 9-A was to test Swirski’s case against *these* ledgers. Only search engines are tested. This book already disagrees with him on at least two other items on his list:

- **VR:** essay 6 scores phantomatics proper OPEN; later-Lem’s EyePhone/DataGlove self-grade OPEN. Swirski counts VR as a hit.
- **SETI’s “failure”:** essay 3 scores the silence OPEN — Wright, Kanodia & Lubar (2018): a drinking glass against the oceans — and restores Lem’s own “Today it is still too early for this” (p. 80). Swirski treats the null as a fulfilled prediction.

Butko, recruited as the independent second witness, then *keeps the scorecard he just called a misunderstanding* and lists ariadnology as fulfilled: “the technology for automatic search of links… is now commonly used in biology (bioinformatics) and library science (search engines)” (p. 99). The collision is Lem *and* Butko against essay 7, not “once” against Lem alone.

**Fix:** Run Swirski’s and Butko’s credited hits through the existing rows. At minimum name VR and SETI as further collisions, and say Butko scored the librarian as a hit.

---

### 7. p. 351 is not the 1992 position

**Location:** “the interview sentence… is the same position the book takes in its own Conclusion, where Lem grounds his warrant not in prophecy but in the claim that the objects he describes already exist in nature and ‘have passed, rather well, an empirical test that is billions of years old’ (p. 351).”

**Problem:** The Conclusion answers a charge of incompetence / Lull’s *Ars Magna*. The warrant is: the “devices” (chromosomal predictors, brains) already exist in Nature, so imitating them is not fantasy. Essay 0 already reads this correctly. Equating “these things exist in biology” with “I am mapping conceptual limits rather than predicting” is this essay’s transposition. The p. 351 quotation is also unregistered for essay 9 (`quotes.tsv` has it only under essay 0); `quotes.py` will not see it.

**Fix:** Do not use p. 351 to underwrite the conceptual-limits thesis. If the sentence stays, register the quote under essay 9 and say what Lem is actually defending.

---

### 8. Object-level / problem-level headers are being used as decoration

**Location:** Ledger subheads “Object level” / “Problem level”. Four “object” rows: (1) not a futurologist, (2) unfalsifiability, (3) cherry-picking / ariadnology, (4) editions. Three “problem” rows: conceptual limits, signposts, category error.

**Problem:** CONTEXT §4: a row is one specific Lem claim against one specific 2026 fact; object = device/date/quantity, problem = the dilemma attached. None of the four “object” rows is an object-level Lem claim. Rows 1 and 5 are the same census (22% vs 43%) paying two HITs — once as “not a futurologist,” once as “mapping conceptual limits.” Essay 0, doing the same meta-job, used “The rules” / “The enterprise.” Here the book’s two-layer vocabulary is applied to a critic’s charges so that “he got the idea” (conceptual limits) and “he got the classification right” (not a futurologist) launder each other.

**Fix:** Rename the blocks (e.g. “The charges” / “What scoring is for”), or put only the ariadnology comparison under object level and the category-error question under problem level. Do not pay the layer split twice.

---

### 9. HIT on “should not be read as a futurologist” is verdict inflation

**Location:** First ledger row, HIT, paid by “22% plain HITs on devices, dates and quantities, with every MISS in the book at that layer.”

**Problem:** Swirski’s “No” is a claim about *scientific* futurology (unequivocal predictions + timelines). A 22% object HIT rate and a 43% problem HIT rate show a forecaster who is better at difficulties than gadgets. That is not the same proposition as “should not be read as a futurologist.” The unfalsifiability row then *needs* him to have been specific enough to fail. The essay HITs “not a futurologist” and RQWA-s “unfalsifiable” with the same corpus. If the sentences were specific enough to falsify, he was doing (some of) what a futurologist does.

**Fix:** HIT, if anything, the narrower claim: he is roughly twice as reliable on problems as on objects, and every outright failure is a device, date, or number. Leave “not a futurologist” OPEN or RQWA — Swirski’s definition is not the same as this book’s scoring rule.

---

### 10. Specialisation MISS used as a falsified forecast

**Location:** “He said technical evolution was producing ‘only narrowly specialized devices’; the direction reversed.”

**Problem:** p. 43 is present-perfect about then-current history: “has so far been moving in a kind of reverse direction… in that it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices.” The next sentences name the way out (self-organizing systems, adaptive self-programming). Essay 2 already scored the continuation as MISS and the named route as RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON. This essay drops “has so far been,” treats a premise as a forecast, and receipts the reversal with `gpt3-2020`. GPT-3 is one general system in 2020; the general-purpose computer, and most of the stack, were already the counterexample, and specialised devices (ASICs, sensors, medical hardware) did not vanish.

**Fix:** Do not use p. 43 as an exhibit of a failed *forecast*. If a second falsification is needed besides freezing / the librarian, use essay 2’s own wording and a receipt that is not a single 2020 model.

---

### 11. “Lived to see machines lacking comprehension provide it” overstates essay 7

**Location:** “specified that no machine lacking comprehension could provide it, and lived to see machines lacking comprehension provide it.” Also: “he rules out precisely the machine that worked.”

**Problem:** Essay 7’s MISS is the *method* clause (“cannot be… based on frequency analysis”; “algorithmic methods… worthless”). Essay 7’s still-open is the *specified* machine: “sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people” — “Counting found what is central; it never sent the right work to the right person.” This essay collapses need, method, and specified machine, then says later-Lem scored “the question and ignor[ed] the answer.” The two-layer rule the paragraph invokes is the one this paragraph breaks.

**Fix:** Keep essay 7’s split. He was wrong that frequency analysis is worthless (MISS, already scored). He was not shown to be wrong that the addressing librarian requires something citation counts do not do (still OPEN). Later-Lem’s “autonomous search engines = ariadnology” then remains a self-grade of the *need*, which is the fair charge.

---

### 12. “Two people who knew the book and the man” / “before any English translation existed”

**Location:** End of the Butko paragraph; the following paragraph repeats the introduction (“Peter Butko, a biophysicist, went through Summa chapter by chapter in 2006, before any English translation existed”).

**Problem:** Swirski interviewed Lem. Nothing in Butko’s chapter claims personal acquaintance. Butko (pp. 83–84) says the opposite of “before any English translation”: the Anglo-American culture already had “two holes,” *A Stanislaw Lem Reader* (1997) — “which devotes considerable space to *Summa*” and prints “Thirty Years Later” — and Livingston’s phantomatics essay. The first *complete* English *Summa* is 2013; that is not what the sentence says. Butko is introduced twice, almost word for word.

**Fix:** One introduction. “Before the complete English *Summa*.” Do not say Butko knew Lem unless a source does.

---

### 13. Unmarked or mis-receipted 2026 claims

Challenge these:

| Claim | Marker | Problem |
|---|---|---|
| “the one domain where 2026 moved most visibly” | none | Generative-media claim with no receipt. |
| “Fifty pedigree mounts copied for half a million dollars each” | `swirski-2022` | Swirski: “**some** fifty,” “**well in excess of** half a million.” Flattened to exact 50 at $500k. The claim row is about Swirski’s argument, not Crestview’s prices (the semantic-receipt hole essay 0 already recorded). |
| “punctuated equilibrium eight years before Eldredge and Gould” | none on that sentence | Presented as Swirski’s credit, but read as fact. PE is 1972; the text being graded is the 1974 fourth edition. “Eight years before” only works if the passage is dated to 1964. Essay 0 scored dating-as-1964 a MISS. |
| “that same year as the Science Citation Index” | `sci-1964` | SCI is 1964. This edition is 1974. Inherited from essay 7; still a 1964/1974 slide. |
| `gpt3-2020` for “the direction reversed” | yes | A 2020 language model does not receipt a reversal of technical evolution as a whole. |
| `book15-census` for “his failures cluster where he was most specific” | yes | The census is a verdict count. Specificity also produced the 11 object-level HITs (Dor Yeshorim, VTOL, etc.). Failures and successes cluster where he was specific. |
| “four MISSes, eleven half-failures, thirty-seven questions” | `book15-census` | Census is 37 OPEN, 26 HIT, 11 RQWA, 4 EARLY, 4 MISS, 3 RAWR. “Half-failures” silently drops 26 HIT, 4 EARLY, and 3 RAWR. |

**Fix:** Mark or cut the unmarked sentences. Quote Swirski’s hedges. Date PE and SCI against the 1974 text or say the passage is being treated as 1964. Do not use the census to underwrite a specificity-causes-failure story.

---

### 14. Missing negative on the case being graded

**Location:** “Where he was worst” is Lem’s self-grade; the only mixed verdicts on Swirski are the straw-man unfalsifiability RQWA and the fiction/ariadnology RAWR. No MISS.

**Problem:** AGENT rule 2 is technically met (there is a RQWA). The honest negative on *this* essay’s subject is sitting in paragraph 3, unused: Swirski’s own scorecard (VR, SETI failure, search engines) is as generous as Lem’s, and this book has already refused two of those credits. Butko’s “Nevertheless, this is a misunderstanding” applies not only to fans’ cards but to Lem’s three self-grade books — context the essay cuts — and Butko then lists ariadnology as fulfilled. The essay finds Lem’s self-grading worst and leaves the critics’ scorecards ungraded.

**Fix:** A row: Swirski/Butko’s credited hit-list vs essays 3, 6, and 7. Verdict RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER or, on SETI-as-failure, MISS against Swirski’s credit (the event this book requires has not happened).

---

### 15. Four MISSes invoked, two named, one of them the collision used later

**Location:** “the reason there are only four MISSes is not that his claims were slippery… Those rows were falsified…: no air-cushion successor, and a reversal of the claim that technology makes only specialised devices.”

**Problem:** The four surviving MISSes are: air-cushion (essay 2), specialisation (essay 2), freezing (essay 6), frequency-analysis librarian (essay 7). The last *is* the ariadnology collision. Naming only the two weakest, then presenting the librarian again as a separate “collision,” makes four look like two plus a different finding.

**Fix:** Name all four, once. Use freezing and the librarian as the specificity exhibits.

---

### 16. Signposts scored HIT while the essay says the metaphor conceals the finding

**Location:** “‘his specific forecasts… could never amount to more than provisional signposts…’” → HIT. “Holds as description. But signposts can be scored, and the scoring is what showed the pattern — his failures cluster where he was most specific, which is a finding the metaphor conceals.”

**Problem:** If the metaphor conceals the result, the row is not HIT. HIT is “described it; it exists/happened substantially as described.” A description the essay immediately retracts is RQWA at best. Combined with finding 13 on “failures cluster.”

**Fix:** RQWA, or cut the row and keep the sentence as prose.

---

### 17. Unregistered quotations (gate-blind)

**Location:** p. 351 “have passed, rather well, an empirical test that is billions of years old”; “only narrowly specialized devices”; Swirski’s “futurology in general and science fiction in particular are not the best places…”; “just in case they do not, no one will be around to give my claims the lie anyway.”; the polo-library clause; the VR/SETI list.

**Problem:** `quotes.tsv` has two essay-9 rows, both p. 367. `secondary.tsv` has the conclusion-block Swirski/Butko lines, not the opening-block ones. AGENT: a quotation with no row is invisible; `verify.sh` will go green over a misquote. This is how essay 5 shipped “we need some restraint.”

**Fix:** Rebuild essay 9’s Lem rows and secondary rows from the HTML after the next edit. Read the unmatched list by eye.

---

### 18. Reader: the skeptic is given totals, then a ledger of critics

**Location:** “What this book found” (85 rows, 22%, 43%, four MISSes) → ledger that grades Swirski and Butko. OPEN row: “a result no admirer would have written and no detractor would have predicted: the questions hold and the answers do not.”

**Problem:** The dinner-test sentence a skeptic of “Lem predicted everything” needs is in the census paragraph. The ledger does not show a single paged Lem claim against a 2026 event. Swirski, Butko, and Zylinska (p. 18: “more interested in signaling certain problems… than in offering any determined visions”) *are* the detractors; “the questions hold and the answers do not” is exactly their prediction. The OPEN cell’s last clause is false.

**Fix:** Put a short table of the four MISSes and a handful of object HITs in the ledger, or point at essays 0–8 for that. Cut “no detractor would have predicted.”

