chapters/05-real-means-useful.html:55:<tr><td>"Believing machines": homeostats with unprogrammed brains in varied bodies and worlds, run to see what metaphysics each grows (pp. 138–140)</td><td>Not run, so far as this essay could establish. What is studied instead is what trained models inherit from human text <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/08-the-invisible-path.html:94:  <a href="09-the-cassandra-syndrome.html">Essay 9 →</a>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:42:<p>The scenario's successor never arrived. Air-cushion vehicles did not take the roads, and at sea they retreated: the car-carrying cross-Channel service closed in October 2000, beaten by a tunnel, and large commercial hovercraft effectively disappeared, leaving one short scheduled route. <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> Wheel drive is untouched. What is displacing the internal combustion engine is a different motor under the same wheels: electric cars reached a quarter of global new-car sales in 2025, more than half in China, so at least three-quarters of new cars still burn fuel. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:47:<p>His p. 43 claim went the other way. When he wrote it, technology had "only been creating narrowly specialized devices"; the direction then reversed, and the systems that now matter most are valued for not being specialised. He named the route — universality via "self-organizing systems… capable of adaptive self-programming" — and what arrived is self-programmed in a loose sense, learned rather than written, but by gradient descent on large corpora rather than the theory he meant. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> His refusal at p. 56 to reserve creative work for people has held: what automated first was writing, drawing and coding. Programmers have not been dispensed with. <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --></p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:56:<tr><td>"One can also imagine further development of mechanical vehicles, which will make wheel drive obsolete. When the current automobile is eventually replaced by an 'air-cushion vehicle'…" (p. 36)</td><td>Wheel drive is untouched. The car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft closed in 2000 and large commercial hovercraft effectively disappeared; the combustion engine is being displaced by an electric motor under the same wheels <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:58:<tr><td>Technical evolution "has so far been moving in a kind of reverse direction to biological evolution in that it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices" (p. 43)</td><td>The direction reversed. The systems that now matter most are valued for being general rather than specialised <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:59:<tr><td>"The potential for universality seems to lie in a further development of the theory of self-organizing systems—which would be capable of adaptive self-programming" (pp. 43–44)</td><td>Universality did come from systems that program themselves rather than being written — but by gradient descent on large corpora, not from the theory of self-organizing systems he meant <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> <!-- CHECK: scaling-laws-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:60:<tr><td>On the claim that only "noncreative mental labor" can be automated: "where is the evidence? Let us be clear about it: there is none, and what is more, there can be none" (p. 56)</td><td>He was right to refuse it. What automated first was writing, drawing and coding, not the manual residue <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/01-the-three-ways-to-be-wrong.html:34:<p>His image for the missing third option is qualified, and the qualification matters: "To a certain degree, the world is like a sick man who believes that either he is going to get better soon or die very quickly and to whom it does not even occur that he can, moaning as he does, and going through some short-term ups and downs, go on living until old age" (p. 29). He then calls the comparison "rather shortsighted" and adds what it leaves out — that a cure brings its own bill. "Liberation from oppression… has its price, while the amount of expected repayment and its period, as well as the method of its delivery, usually come as a surprise" (p. 29), his example being radioactive waste, "which we still do not know what we should do about" (p. 29). The chapter ends by refusing endings: history "does not know, and I hope will not know, such definitive closures or 'final ends'" (p. 30). The baseline: Blackett of 1948, Thirring of 1946, Bellamy from the 1880s, a balance of fear about fifteen years old. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch1 --> This translation follows the altered fourth edition of 1974. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
chapters/01-the-three-ways-to-be-wrong.html:41:<p>The taxonomy itself holds up where it can be tested against this book's own scoring, though each mode needs a specific case rather than a mood of its own. The straight line is the best evidenced: essay 2 records the scenario in which the car is replaced by an air-cushion vehicle — the impressive machine of its moment, extended forward — and air-cushion vehicles retreated to a marginal role <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> while what is displacing the combustion engine kept the wheels, the roads and the shape. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --> The sudden turn is evidenced by third options: essay 7 finds that what automated science was neither of the two routes Lem posed but a formalism that executes, <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> and essay 8 finds that selection on heredity, whose partner-screening form he described correctly, also acquired a second and unforeseen venue once the decision could be moved past conception. <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> The closed schema is evidenced by an outcome that refused both endings: essay 6 records headsets that neither replaced reality nor disappeared, but sold, peaked and settled. <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --></p>
chapters/01-the-three-ways-to-be-wrong.html:54:<tr><td>The straight line: "we are always inclined to extend the course of new technologies into the future by means of straight lines" (p. 28)</td><td>Confirmed on his own work by this book's strictest verdict: the air-cushion successor was the impressive machine of its moment extended forward, and the wheels stayed <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-agy.md:17:*   **Location:** "...the direction reversed. `<!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 -->`"
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:32:<p>Two facts about the text govern everything else. First, the editions. <em>Summa</em> appeared in Polish in 1964 and was reissued that year, with altered editions in 1967 and 1974; this translation follows the fourth edition of 1974, chosen as "the most mature and up-to-date", and Butko's description is quoted approvingly: "<em>Summa</em> is essentially a work in progress: Lem corrected mistakes and updated his thinking, based in part on feedback from readers who are often scientists and experts in their respective fields" (p. 20). <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> The book being graded is therefore a revised book. Second, Lem graded himself, and graded generously. His later essays "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" update the examples "with their later scientific equivalents (e.g., synthetic biology and virtual reality devices such as Eye Phone and Data Glove)", and Zylinska is explicit about their purpose: "Those latter efforts were aimed at demonstrating how many of the inventions and discoveries Lem did manage to predict correctly in <em>Summa</em>" (p. 21). Neither essay is in this English edition. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:39:<p>Eight essays grade Lem, in eighty-five rows. The pooled distribution is unflattering to the enterprise — <strong>37 OPEN, 26 HIT, 11 RIGHT QUESTION WRONG ANSWER, 4 EARLY, 4 MISS, 3 RIGHT ANSWER WRONG REASON</strong> — and the modal verdict, by a distance, is that the question is not settled. <!-- CHECK: book15-census --> But the pooled number is the wrong number, and the rule that made it wrong is the one this book kept most strictly: object level and problem level are scored in separate rows. Split them and the finding appears.</p>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:40:<p>At object level — devices, dates, quantities — there are 50 rows and 11 plain HITs, a rate of 22 percent, with all four MISSes and all four EARLYs. At problem level — the shape of a difficulty, what a thing will cost, who will decide — there are 35 rows and 15 HITs, 43 percent, and <strong>no MISSes at all.</strong> <!-- CHECK: book15-census --> Every outright failure in this book is a device, a date or a number. Not one is a question. Lem is roughly twice as reliable about what will be hard as about what will be built, which is the result the two-layer method exists to produce and the reason it was worth the bookkeeping.</p>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:42:<p>The vocabulary had seven words and one was never used. Not a single row — not one of the ninety-three — earned <em>DISSOLVED</em>, the verdict for a question that stopped being a question. <!-- CHECK: book15-census --> That is a fact about this corpus rather than a law: eight chapters were graded and the ninth, on art, is missing, so the sample is the part of Lem that was already about engineering. Within it, nothing he framed became meaningless.</p>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:55:<tr><td>Object level and problem level scored in separate rows, so that "he got the idea" never launders "he got the thing wrong"</td><td>The most productive rule in the book. It is what produced the finding: 22% plain HITs on devices and dates against 43% on problems, and every MISS at object level <!-- CHECK: book15-census --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:57:<tr><td>A fixed seven-word verdict vocabulary, one word per row</td><td>Adequate — no row in ninety-three needed an eighth word — and oversized: <em>DISSOLVED</em> was never earned once, on a corpus that is missing its one chapter about art <!-- CHECK: book15-census --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:59:<tr><td>Grade against the state of the art he extrapolated from, dated</td><td>The book dated its own corpus wrongly for most of its writing: "1964" throughout, for a text that is the fourth edition of 1974 <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:61:<tr><td>That a book of this kind can be scored at all — against Zylinska's reading that Lem was "signaling certain problems and posing questions" rather than "offering any determined visions" (p. 18)</td><td>Yes, and the scoring supports her reading rather than defeating it. Where he named a device, a number or an order, the rows close and he loses; where he described a difficulty, they close and he wins <!-- CHECK: book15-census --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:62:<tr><td>That an outside verdict is worth more than the author's own — his later essays being "aimed at demonstrating how many of the inventions and discoveries Lem did manage to predict correctly" (p. 21)</td><td>Cannot be tested here. "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" are not in this edition, so the two gradings cannot be compared line by line <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html:79:<li><strong>Chapter 9 and the afterwords.</strong> "Art and Technology" is absent from this translation, and so are "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> — the two texts in which Lem scores himself, and therefore the only direct check on whether this book's verdicts are harsher than the author's for good reasons. What would close it: the Polish or Russian text of chapter 9, and either afterword.</li>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:44:<p>On the second sieve the record splits, and not as the chapter expects. The deluge arrived — from ordinary human production, with no farm anywhere near it. Publication doubles every nine years; <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record, mostly from compromised review and paper mills; <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> and a 2025 analysis found the brokerages selling authorship in mass-produced papers large, resilient and growing faster than the legitimate literature — his "works that are only produced to obtain an academic degree or to satisfy someone's ambition", industrialised. <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --> He expected that pressure to strengthen selection at the gate; in the venues these papers reach, it did not. Meanwhile the addressing technology he ruled out was built first and worked: Garfield's Science Citation Index appeared in 1964, the year of this book, indexing 613 journals by counting citations — the "frequency analysis" he called worthless. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> In 1998 PageRank extended citation analysis to hyperlinks, citing Garfield. <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --> What was never built is the machine he specified: one "sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people" (p. 367).</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:57:<tr><td>"A machine librarian… cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis"; "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless" for selecting from the avalanche (p. 367)</td><td>Wrong on the method: counting citations launched that same year as the Science Citation Index, became the standard way to find scientific literature, and was extended to the web by PageRank <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
