drafts/reviews/01-codex.err:1983:chapters/07-information-farming.html-44-<p>On the second sieve the record splits, and not as the chapter expects. The deluge arrived — from ordinary human production, with no farm anywhere near it. Publication doubles every nine years; <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record, mostly from compromised review and paper mills; <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> and a 2025 analysis found the brokerages selling authorship in mass-produced papers large, resilient and growing faster than the legitimate literature — his "works that are only produced to obtain an academic degree or to satisfy someone's ambition", industrialised. <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --> He expected that pressure to strengthen selection at the gate; in the venues these papers reach, it did not. Meanwhile the addressing technology he ruled out was built first and worked: Garfield's Science Citation Index appeared in 1964, the year of this book, indexing 613 journals by counting citations — the "frequency analysis" he called worthless. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> In 1998 PageRank extended citation analysis to hyperlinks, citing Garfield. <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --> What was never built is the machine he specified: one "sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people" (p. 367).</p>
drafts/reviews/01-codex.err:1995:chapters/07-information-farming.html-57-<tr><td>"A machine librarian… cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis"; "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless" for selecting from the avalanche (p. 367)</td><td>Wrong on the method: counting citations launched that same year as the Science Citation Index, became the standard way to find scientific literature, and was extended to the web by PageRank <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/01-codex.err:2047:chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html-43-<p>And the book got its own date wrong. Every essay was written against "the 1964 baseline", and the corpus is the fourth edition of 1974. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> For a book whose entire method is that the date of a claim determines how it is scored, this is the worst error in it. It mattered concretely at least once: essay 2 credited Lem with a mechanism illustrated by an aircraft that had been in service for five years before the edition the essay was quoting. The caveat is now in all eight essays.</p>
drafts/reviews/01-codex.err:2058:chapters/00-how-to-grade-a-prophet.html-77-<li><strong>Chapter 9 and the afterwords.</strong> "Art and Technology" is absent from this translation, and so are "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> — the two texts in which Lem scores himself, and therefore the only direct check on whether this book's verdicts are harsher than the author's for good reasons. What would close it: the Polish or Russian text of chapter 9, and either afterword.</li>
