drafts/reviews/04-codex.err:616:    42	<p>And the boxes were not made of what he thought. He had the perceptron and gave it a clerk's job — reading text <em>into</em> the real machine. What happened is that reading text at scale became the way the competence itself was grown: language-model loss falls as a smooth power law in data, parameters and compute, <!-- CHECK: scaling-laws-2020 --> and a model trained on web-scale text does tasks it was never trained for. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> The chapter never treats written language, at scale, as the medium a box's competence is grown from. That is the omission this essay has to record — not a failed forecast, a route he had no way to see — and it decides what the boxes are made of, and so what habits they arrive with. Meanwhile the megabyte bomb has not gone off on his schedule: the scientific literature kept doubling roughly every nine years through the turn of the century, and the S-fold he placed between 1994 and 2034 has not visibly arrived. <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --></p>
drafts/reviews/04-codex.err:625:    51	<tr><td>Perceptrons "cannot recognize, say, human faces; neither can they … 'read texts.' But they are an important step" toward machines that will (p. 153)</td><td>Deep networks recognise faces and read text; reading text at scale became the training route itself (GPT-3) <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
