drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:886:<p>Two facts about the text govern everything else. First, the editions. <em>Summa</em> appeared in Polish in 1964 and was reissued that year, with altered editions in 1967 and 1974; this translation follows the fourth edition of 1974, chosen as "the most mature and up-to-date", and Butko's description is quoted approvingly: "<em>Summa</em> is essentially a work in progress: Lem corrected mistakes and updated his thinking, based in part on feedback from readers who are often scientists and experts in their respective fields" (p. 20). <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> The book being graded is therefore a revised book. Second, Lem graded himself, and graded generously. His later essays "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" update the examples "with their later scientific equivalents (e.g., synthetic biology and virtual reality devices such as Eye Phone and Data Glove)", and Zylinska is explicit about their purpose: "Those latter efforts were aimed at demonstrating how many of the inventions and discoveries Lem did manage to predict correctly in <em>Summa</em>" (p. 21). Neither essay is in this English edition. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:897:<p>And the book got its own date wrong. Every essay was written against "the 1964 baseline", and the corpus is the fourth edition of 1974. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> For a book whose entire method is that the date of a claim determines how it is scored, this is the worst error in it. It mattered concretely at least once: essay 2 credited Lem with a mechanism illustrated by an aircraft that had been in service for five years before the edition the essay was quoting. The caveat is now in all eight essays.</p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:911:<tr><td>Grade against the state of the art he extrapolated from, dated</td><td>The book dated the corpus wrongly for most of its writing: "1964" throughout, for a text that is the fourth edition of 1974 <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:931:<li><strong>Chapter 9 and the afterwords.</strong> "Art and Technology" is absent from this translation, and so are "Twenty Years Later" and "Thirty Years Later" <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --> — the two texts in which Lem scores himself, and therefore the only direct check on whether this book's verdicts are harsher than the author's for good reasons. What would close it: the Polish or Russian text of chapter 9, and either afterword.</li>
