drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2357:    34	<p>His image for the missing third option is qualified, and the qualification matters: "To a certain degree, the world is like a sick man who believes that either he is going to get better soon or die very quickly and to whom it does not even occur that he can, moaning as he does, and going through some short-term ups and downs, go on living until old age" (p. 29). He then calls the comparison "rather shortsighted" and adds what it leaves out — that a cure brings its own bill. "Liberation from oppression… has its price, while the amount of expected repayment and its period, as well as the method of its delivery, usually come as a surprise" (p. 29), his example being radioactive waste, "which we still do not know what we should do about" (p. 29). The chapter ends by refusing endings: history "does not know, and I hope will not know, such definitive closures or 'final ends'" (p. 30). The baseline: Blackett of 1948, Thirring of 1946, Bellamy from the 1880s, a balance of fear about fifteen years old. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch1 --> This translation follows the altered fourth edition of 1974. <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2364:    41	<p>The taxonomy itself holds up where it can be tested against this book's own scoring, though each mode needs a specific case rather than a mood of its own. The straight line is the best evidenced: essay 2 records the scenario in which the car is replaced by an air-cushion vehicle — the impressive machine of its moment, extended forward — and air-cushion vehicles retreated to a marginal role <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> while what is displacing the combustion engine kept the wheels, the roads and the shape. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --> The sudden turn is evidenced by third options: essay 7 finds that what automated science was neither of the two routes Lem posed but a formalism that executes, <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> and essay 8 finds that selection on heredity, whose partner-screening form he described correctly, also acquired a second and unforeseen venue once the decision could be moved past conception. <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> The closed schema is evidenced by an outcome that refused both endings: essay 6 records headsets that neither replaced reality nor disappeared, but sold, peaked and settled. <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --></p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2377:    54	<tr><td>The straight line: "we are always inclined to extend the course of new technologies into the future by means of straight lines" (p. 28)</td><td>Confirmed on his own work by this book's strictest verdict: the air-cushion successor was the impressive machine of its moment extended forward, and the wheels stayed <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2418:    44	<p>On the second sieve the record splits, and not as the chapter expects. The deluge arrived — from ordinary human production, with no farm anywhere near it. Publication doubles every nine years; <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record, mostly from compromised review and paper mills; <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> and a 2025 analysis found the brokerages selling authorship in mass-produced papers large, resilient and growing faster than the legitimate literature — his "works that are only produced to obtain an academic degree or to satisfy someone's ambition", industrialised. <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --> He expected that pressure to strengthen selection at the gate; in the venues these papers reach, it did not. Meanwhile the addressing technology he ruled out was built first and worked: Garfield's Science Citation Index appeared in 1964, the year of this book, indexing 613 journals by counting citations — the "frequency analysis" he called worthless. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> In 1998 PageRank extended citation analysis to hyperlinks, citing Garfield. <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --> What was never built is the machine he specified: one "sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people" (p. 367).</p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2431:    57	<tr><td>"A machine librarian… cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis"; "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless" for selecting from the avalanche (p. 367)</td><td>Wrong on the method: counting citations launched that same year as the Science Citation Index, became the standard way to find scientific literature, and was extended to the web by PageRank <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2470:    41	<p>The taxonomy itself holds up where it can be tested against this book's own scoring, though each mode needs a specific case rather than a mood of its own. The straight line is the best evidenced: essay 2 records the scenario in which the car is replaced by an air-cushion vehicle — the impressive machine of its moment, extended forward — and air-cushion vehicles retreated to a marginal role <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> while what is displacing the combustion engine kept the wheels, the roads and the shape. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --> The sudden turn is evidenced by third options: essay 7 finds that what automated science was neither of the two routes Lem posed but a formalism that executes, <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> and essay 8 finds that selection on heredity, whose partner-screening form he described correctly, also acquired a second and unforeseen venue once the decision could be moved past conception. <!-- CHECK: embryo-screening-2024 --> The closed schema is evidenced by an outcome that refused both endings: essay 6 records headsets that neither replaced reality nor disappeared, but sold, peaked and settled. <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --></p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2483:    54	<tr><td>The straight line: "we are always inclined to extend the course of new technologies into the future by means of straight lines" (p. 28)</td><td>Confirmed on his own work by this book's strictest verdict: the air-cushion successor was the impressive machine of its moment extended forward, and the wheels stayed <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2506:    42	<p>The scenario's successor never arrived. Air-cushion vehicles did not take the roads, and at sea they retreated: the car-carrying cross-Channel service closed in October 2000, beaten by a tunnel, and large commercial hovercraft effectively disappeared, leaving one short scheduled route. <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> Wheel drive is untouched. What is displacing the internal combustion engine is a different motor under the same wheels: electric cars reached a quarter of global new-car sales in 2025, more than half in China, so at least three-quarters of new cars still burn fuel. <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2511:    47	<p>His p. 43 claim went the other way. When he wrote it, technology had "only been creating narrowly specialized devices"; the direction then reversed, and the systems that now matter most are valued for not being specialised. He named the route — universality via "self-organizing systems… capable of adaptive self-programming" — and what arrived is self-programmed in a loose sense, learned rather than written, but by gradient descent on large corpora rather than the theory he meant. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> His refusal at p. 56 to reserve creative work for people has held: what automated first was writing, drawing and coding. Programmers have not been dispensed with. <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --></p>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2520:    56	<tr><td>"One can also imagine further development of mechanical vehicles, which will make wheel drive obsolete. When the current automobile is eventually replaced by an 'air-cushion vehicle'…" (p. 36)</td><td>Wheel drive is untouched. The car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft closed in 2000 and large commercial hovercraft effectively disappeared; the combustion engine is being displaced by an electric motor under the same wheels <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2522:    58	<tr><td>Technical evolution "has so far been moving in a kind of reverse direction to biological evolution in that it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices" (p. 43)</td><td>The direction reversed. The systems that now matter most are valued for being general rather than specialised <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2523:    59	<tr><td>"The potential for universality seems to lie in a further development of the theory of self-organizing systems—which would be capable of adaptive self-programming" (pp. 43–44)</td><td>Universality did come from systems that program themselves rather than being written — but by gradient descent on large corpora, not from the theory of self-organizing systems he meant <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> <!-- CHECK: scaling-laws-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2524:    60	<tr><td>On the claim that only "noncreative mental labor" can be automated: "where is the evidence? Let us be clear about it: there is none, and what is more, there can be none" (p. 56)</td><td>He was right to refuse it. What automated first was writing, drawing and coding, not the manual residue <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/00-codex.err:2606:    55	<tr><td>"Believing machines": homeostats with unprogrammed brains in varied bodies and worlds, run to see what metaphysics each grows (pp. 138–140)</td><td>Not run, so far as this essay could establish. What is studied instead is what trained models inherit from human text <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/07-codex.r1.md:19:| Agy 8 — `gpt3-2020` does not receipt tool use | **CONFIRMED** | GPT-3 pays only the text-trained competence clause; tool action, search loops, and opacity require other adjacent receipts. |
drafts/reviews/07-codex.r1.md:72:**Location:** problem-level row “A third language … EARLY”; prose, “grown outside a brain, from written language, now acts on the world through tools … outputs we can use and cannot follow. `gpt3-2020`”
drafts/reviews/07-codex.r1.md:74:This is an object-level forecast of a system/language, not a problem-level dilemma. Move it to Object level. EARLY is plausible, but the evidence must be split. `gpt3-2020` supports web-scale text training and few-shot behavior; it does not support tool use, search loops, causal action, or opacity. `ai-scientist-2026` supports an agentic tool-using pipeline, and `funsearch-2023` supports the search-loop generator, but the prose does not place those markers on this sentence and the ledger omits FunSearch’s marker. “Cannot follow” needs an interpretability receipt. Agy’s chronology language is imprecise—the essay does not claim that GPT-3 itself used tools—but its receipt-mismatch finding is correct.
drafts/reviews/07-codex.r1.md:84:- `baseline-1964`, `scaling-laws-2020`, and `gpt3-2020` are registered as essay-4 claims and reused here for materially broader essay-7 claims. The checker ignores the essay column and semantic drift.
