drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:977:<p>Swirski opens on cloned polo horses, because nobody forecast them. Fifty pedigree mounts copied for half a million dollars each is a future nobody wrote, and no library of forecasts contains "a single word about the cutthroat world of professional polo, the lifestyles of moneyed elites, and a ground-breaking Latin American business enterprise". <!-- CHECK: swirski-2022 --> The lesson he draws is general: that "futurology in general and science fiction in particular are not the best places to look for enlightenment about the future", and that this applies to Lem like anyone else.</p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:980:<p>The sharper observation is about the author. Lem graded himself, repeatedly — in <em>The Mystery of the Chinese Room</em> (1996), <em>The Megabit Bomb</em> (1999) and <em>The Blink of an Eye</em> (2000) — and among the successes he claimed were "the adaptive morphology of the human brain, biocomputing, and autonomous search engines", the last of which he called "ariadnology: the science of threads". <!-- CHECK: lem-self-grades --> Swirski's verdict on that habit is unsentimental: "he is not above cherry-picking his fiction for predictive hits."</p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:981:<p>The conclusion is flat. "Should Lem be read, not to say revered, as a futurologist?" — "in any proper—which is to say scientific—understanding of the term, the answer has to be No." But the sentence that follows is the one that matters, because it is Lem's own, from Swirski's 1992 interviews: "more than in predicting the future he has always been more interested in mapping the conceptual limits of science and its technological offshoots". Against that, "his specific forecasts—whether in Summa or in his science-driven fiction—could never amount to more than provisional signposts". <!-- CHECK: swirski-2022 --></p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:982:<p>And the same verdict arrives independently, six years earlier and from a scientist rather than a critic. Peter Butko, closing his chapter-by-chapter reading of Summa, notes the same habit — "Some read the book as a list of predictions and keep little score cards, where they note the fulfilled prophecies as well as the perceived failures" — and rejects it in one word: "Nevertheless, this is a misunderstanding." <em>Summa</em>, he writes, "is not a prognostication almanac, and its author is not Nostradamus or Jules Verne", because "The aim of Summa was not to predict particular gadgets but rather to explore the conceptual foundations of the two evolutions and their relations to society and civilization" (pp. 98–99). His conclusion about the hits is the sharpest sentence either scholar writes: "Individual predictions that have come true are rather byproducts of Lem's creative thinking." <!-- CHECK: butko-2006 --> Two people who knew the book and the man, arriving separately at the same objection to the exercise this book performs.</p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:983:<p>One more witness, from the same editor's earlier volume. Peter Butko, a biophysicist, went through Summa chapter by chapter in 2006, before any English translation existed, and described it as a book Lem "corrected and updated" on reader feedback — the judgement Zylinska cites when she explains why she translated the fourth edition of 1974 rather than the first of 1964. <!-- CHECK: butko-2006 --> <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:988:<p>The ledgers agree with Swirski, and they agree with him more precisely than his own argument does. Across eight essays there are eighty-five graded rows. Split by layer — the separation this book insisted on from the start — the object-level rows, which grade devices, dates and quantities, return 11 plain HITs from 50, a rate of 22 percent, and carry every MISS in the book. The problem-level rows, which grade what will be hard, what it will cost and who will decide, return 15 from 35, a rate of 43 percent, and carry <strong>no MISSes at all.</strong> <!-- CHECK: book15-census --></p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:990:<p>But half the case does not survive contact with the same evidence. The unfalsifiability charge — heads I win, tails you lose — does not describe Summa's object-level claims, and this book is the demonstration. He said the car would be replaced by an air-cushion vehicle; it was not. <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> He said technical evolution was producing "only narrowly specialized devices"; the direction reversed. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> Those rows were falsified because they were specific enough to falsify, and the reason there are only four MISSes is not that his claims were slippery but that most of them are not yet decidable, which is a different failing and shows up as thirty-seven OPEN rows. Loose forecasting is the sin Swirski names; the sin this book actually found is a man being specific about the wrong things.</p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:991:<p>The two scorecards collide once, and the collision is instructive. Lem claimed autonomous search engines as a success and named them with his own coinage, ariadnology. <!-- CHECK: lem-self-grades --> Essay 7 graded the passage that coinage comes from and scored it a MISS — because in the same note he rules out precisely the machine that worked, insisting that a machine librarian "cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis", and that "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless when it comes to making selections from among the 'avalanche of information'" (p. 367). Counting citations was the method he called worthless; it launched that same year as the Science Citation Index and became PageRank. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --> He named the need and got the mechanism backwards, then claimed the need as a hit.</p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:992:<p>Which is what an author's scorecard does, and why the disagreement matters more than either verdict. Swirski's charge of cherry-picking is not a charge about honesty; it is a charge about what self-assessment is for. This book's totals — four MISSes, eleven half-failures, thirty-seven questions it cannot settle <!-- CHECK: book15-census --> — are not the shape a self-assessment produces, and they are not flattering enough to be worth writing if flattery were the point.</p>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1001:<tr><td>Lem should not be read as a futurologist: "in any proper—which is to say scientific—understanding of the term, the answer has to be No" (Swirski, 2022)</td><td>Supported, and more precisely than argued: 22% plain HITs on devices, dates and quantities, with every MISS in the book at that layer <!-- CHECK: book15-census --> <!-- CHECK: swirski-2022 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1002:<tr><td>Lem's forecasts are unfalsifiable by construction — "Heads I win, tails you lose"</td><td>Not for the text this book graded. Four object-level rows were falsified outright because they were specific: no air-cushion successor, and a reversal of the claim that technology makes only specialised devices <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1003:<tr><td>Lem "is not above cherry-picking his fiction for predictive hits", and claimed autonomous search engines — "ariadnology" — among his successes <!-- CHECK: lem-self-grades --></td><td>The one place the scorecards can be compared, the author's is too kind: the note that coins ariadnology also rules out the method that worked, calling algorithmic selection "worthless" the year citation indexing launched <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --></td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1004:<tr><td>Butko: Summa is a book Lem "corrected and updated" edition by edition, which is why the standard English text is the fourth edition of 1974 <!-- CHECK: butko-2006 --></td><td>Correct, and this book got it wrong for most of its writing — grading a 1974 text while calling it 1964, an error essay 0 scores against the method <!-- CHECK: summa-editions --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1006:<tr><td>Lem to Swirski, 1992: "more than in predicting the future he has always been more interested in mapping the conceptual limits of science and its technological offshoots"</td><td>The ledgers say so independently: 43% HITs at problem level against 22% at object level, and not one outright failure about a difficulty <!-- CHECK: book15-census --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1007:<tr><td>"his specific forecasts… could never amount to more than provisional signposts on the road to our technologically turbocharged future"</td><td>Holds as description. But signposts can be scored, and the scoring is what showed the pattern — his failures cluster where he was most specific, which is a finding the metaphor conceals <!-- CHECK: book15-census --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1008:<tr><td>The implied charge in "Some fans even keep score cards" — that grading a thinker like this is a category error</td><td>Unresolved, and this book cannot settle it about itself. What it can say is that the exercise produced a result no admirer would have written and no detractor would have predicted: the questions hold and the answers do not <!-- CHECK: book15-census --> <!-- CHECK: swirski-2022 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1023:<li><strong>Whether the author's scorecard is generous throughout.</strong> One comparison was possible here, and it went against him. What would close it: the three later collections — <em>The Mystery of the Chinese Room</em>, <em>The Megabit Bomb</em>, <em>The Blink of an Eye</em> — none of which is held here, so every claim in them reaches this book through Swirski's summary <!-- CHECK: lem-self-grades --></li>
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1061:**Location:** Meta description: “Lem’s own translator says he should not be read as a futurologist”. Lede: “made by the man who translated Lem, interviewed him at length…”. Claim row `swirski-2022`: “Peter Swirski, one of Lem’s translators”.
drafts/reviews/09-codex.err:1148:**Problem:** p. 43 is present-perfect about then-current history: “has so far been moving in a kind of reverse direction… in that it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices.” The next sentences name the way out (self-organizing systems, adaptive self-programming). Essay 2 already scored the continuation as MISS and the named route as RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON. This essay drops “has so far been,” treats a premise as a forecast, and receipts the reversal with `gpt3-2020`. GPT-3 is one general system in 2020; the general-purpose computer, and most of the stack, were already the counterexample, and specialised devices (ASICs, sensors, medical hardware) did not vanish.
