drafts/reviews/07-codex.err:497:<p>The second sieve is where the record is worst. Note 8's deluge arrived on schedule. Publication doubles every nine years; <!-- CHECK: bornmann-mutz-2015 --> more than 10,000 papers were retracted in 2023, a record, mostly from compromised review and paper mills; <!-- CHECK: retractions-2023 --> and a 2025 analysis found the brokerages selling authorship in mass-produced papers large, resilient and growing faster than the legitimate literature — his "works that are only produced to obtain an academic degree or to satisfy someone's ambition", industrialised. <!-- CHECK: papermills-2025 --> He expected that pressure to tighten the gate. It loosened. What arrived on time was the addressing technology, by the method he ruled out: Garfield's Science Citation Index appeared in 1964, the year of this book, indexing 613 journals by counting citations — the "frequency analysis" he called worthless. <!-- CHECK: sci-1964 --> It ran scientific search for fifty years, and in 1998 the same idea, generalised to hyperlinks and crediting Garfield, became PageRank. <!-- CHECK: pagerank-1998 --></p>
