43-Cloning has been, of course, a staple of all manner of futuristic fictions
44-and futurological predictions for a very long time. But, as my example
45-demonstrates, that future is already today, although it is a future unanticipated by even the most pedigreed futurologists. Do not believe me? Take
46-a walk through the libraries filled with forecasts from would-be prophets of
47-the things to come. Try as you might, you will not find a single word about
48:the cutthroat world of professional polo, the lifestyles of moneyed elites, and
49-a ground-breaking Latin American business enterprise.
50-If there is a lesson in all this—and I think there is—it is that futurology in
51-general and science fiction in particular are not the best places to look for
52-enlightenment about the future. And, given that Stanislaw Lem is revered
53-internationally not only as a writer of science fiction but also futurologist,
--
245-Guess what? Given my ridiculous timeline and the open-ended nature of my
246-claims—exploration, exhaustion, sorting out, and continuation are all open
247-to interpretation—it is a pretty safe bet that all these futuristic wonders will
248-have come to pass. And just in case they do not, no one will be around to
249-give my claims the lie anyway.
250:Heads I win, tails you lose.
251-Of course, Lem is more than just a writer of fiction and among his numerous volumes of nonfiction there is a volume of futurological speculations
252-par excellence, the legendary Summa Technologiae. Given the farsightedness of Lem’s futuristic scenarios, over the years more than one
253-commentator has succumbed to the temptation of likening him to a latterday Nostradamus and Summa as a book of prophecies. In this they have
254-followed in the footsteps of Lem himself who regularly returned to his predictions to evaluate their accuracy.4
255-This is not to deny that Summa is remarkably savvy in some of its deliberations. On the margins of his discussion of autoevolution, for example,
256:Lem appears to suggest the theory of punctuated equilibrium a full eight
257-years before it entered the contemporary evolutionary lexicon via Eldredge
258-and Gould. He also appears to anticipate—albeit under different names—
259-artificial emotion, virtual reality, search engines, nanobots, bionics, and not
260:least the failure of Frank Drake’s SETI to unearth evidence of intelligence
261:elsewhere in the cosmos. Some fans even keep score cards, with check
262-marks next to the fulfilled prognoses and question marks next to the perceived flops. “How come he did not foresee the Internet?” runs one common
263-complaint. Well, he did, answer the apologists, only not in Summa but in
264-a 1961 novel Return from the Stars (in the guise of a global Infor network).
265-Lem himself assessed the predictive accuracy of his futurological opus
266-magnum in three later collections of essays: The Mystery of the Chinese
267-Room (1996), The Megabit Bomb (1999), and The Blink of an Eye (2000).
268-As he scrupulously and sometimes smugly pointed out, among his successful
269:predictions from the early 1960s were also the adaptive morphology of the
270-human brain, biocomputing, and autonomous search engines (Lem refers to
271-the latter as ariadnology: the science of threads).
272-Needless to say, Lem’s cognitive ambitions were never confined to nonfiction. In his early novel The Magellan Nebula (1955), for example, he actually took an unusual step of appending a Pocket Lexicon of Scientific
273-Terms to guide his readers through the thicket of science in the novel. Lem
274-glossed no less than thirty two terms, chiefly from astrophysics—including
--
295-fact that in the novel trions are invented in the twenty-seventh century. In
296-reality, of course, it took less than thirty years after The Magellan Nebula
297-for the first CDs to appear on the market. To his credit, Lem would confess
298-to being completely taken aback by the speed of development of computers
299-and related technologies. His admission of failure, however, does not change
300:the fact that he is not above cherry-picking his fiction for predictive hits that
301-supposedly shore up his clairvoyance.
302-But among this patchwork of hits and misses, some of the misses are so
303-egregious as to raise a big question mark over Lem’s scientific credibility. An
304-instructive example comes from his signature book of fictional futurology,
305-The Futurological Congress (1971). In our own world of the year 2022, with
