374-Combined with a long list of predictive misses and scientific errors in
375-Lem’s fiction, this statement bring us to back to the central question. Should
376:Lem be read, not to say revered, as a futurologist? On balance evidence suggests that in any proper—which is to say scientific—understanding of the
377-term, the answer has to be No. This is not, however, as damning as it might
378-seem. As Lem himself told me during our extensive 1992 interviews, more
379-than in predicting the future he has always been more interested in mapping
380-the conceptual limits of science and its technological offshoots. Next to his
381-stunning visionary imagination, his specific forecasts—whether in Summa
382:or in his science-driven fiction—could never amount to more than provisional signposts on the road to our technologically turbocharged future.
