 succeeded in 253ms:
chapters/07-information-farming.html-31-<p>Then the specification: "We are to invent a device that will gather information, generalize it in the same way the scientist does, and present the results of this inquiry to experts. The device collects facts, generalizes them, and checks the validity of this generalization by applying it to a new set of facts" (p. 243). And immediately: "Our device thus produces theories" (p. 243) — theories in the strict sense he then spells out, structural equivalents of a phenomenon whose transformations track its trajectory.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-32-<p>The design follows. A fertilised egg computes an organism without writing anything down: "Could we not take up 'information farming,' cross-breed bits of information with one another, and initiate their 'growth' so that we eventually obtain a 'mature organism' in the form of scientific theory?" (pp. 251–252). The carriers are "large molecules of synthetic polymers" (p. 263) whose development is <em>isodynamic</em> with the phenomenon studied — not a picture of it, a process running alongside it. They pass through sieves, each "connected (e.g., by means of automatic manipulators, perceptrons, etc.) to a real phenomenon" (p. 264) whose actual state is encoded as molecular specks and crashed against the carriers. Those that predicted it survive; repeat, and you climb from "a theory of the examined class" toward "a theory of the class of classes" (p. 265). The law installed in place of biological fitness is the crux: "that which expresses the environment most adequately survives" (p. 259).</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-33-<p>He states Ashby's position fairly first — "new theories count for nothing… while the only things that really matter are elimination and selection" (pp. 260–261) — and does not dispute it; what he rejects is the <em>source</em> of the variants: "Ashby thinks that one should start from the greatest diversity and then 'filter' it gradually. We, in turn, want to start from significant yet not enormous diversity" (p. 261). He is explicit that formal generation and empirical selection already work as a pair: "Mathematicians are thus a generator of diversity, whereas empiricists are the selector postulated by Ashby" (p. 261). His bet against formalism is a bet about <em>representation</em>: write fetal development out in chemical notation and the formulae run "in the realm of quadrillions. There would not be enough surface across all the oceans and the entire mainland for it" (p. 251). Hence the choice between mathematics "encoded in the binary elements of large electron machines" and "the kind that actualizes the fertilized egg without any formalism whatsoever" (p. 251), and hence the rule at p. 263: "We need to represent processes with some other processes, not with formal signs."</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-34-<p>And then there is note 8 — three pages of small type at the back of the book (pp. 365–367), and it takes most of the design apart. A farm, he writes, "can actually exacerbate, rather than diminish, a crisis resulting from the excess of information" (p. 365), because it "only offers alternative ways of proceeding; it reveals what can be achieved but not whether we should proceed at all", and "Such a decision-making process must not be mechanized" (p. 365). Worse, one running too far ahead of the civilisation that started it produces information that "would not only be useless but also incomprehensible," at which point "the criteria for eliminating irrelevant information would disappear" and it becomes "a 'bomb'—if not a megabyte then at least a gigabyte one" (p. 366). The condition on the apparatus is exact: "As long as the selectors at the information farm are under the active influence of intelligent beings, they are capable of selecting information effectively" (p. 366). So there are two selectors in Lem: one wired to the phenomenon, deciding what is true, one wired to the addressee, deciding what is worth saying.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:35:<p>He then applies that second sieve to the literature of his own day. Libraries grow exponentially and abstracts cannot keep up, so methodology "should give way to 'ariadnology'—which would stand for a guide to the labyrinth of the already assembled knowledge" (p. 367). He rules out the obvious machine: "A machine librarian, sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people, cannot be a device that lacks 'comprehension' and that is based on frequency analysis." He knows the attempts exist — "counting how often they get mentioned by experts in the bibliographies of their publications… are already under way" — and dismisses them: "algorithmic methods still turn out to be worthless when it comes to making selections from among the 'avalanche of information'" (p. 367). The correction, he expects, comes from publishing, hedged: "It seems that the crisis of information distribution will lead to the strengthening of publication criteria in the future so that their original selection prevents the flooding of the professional market with insignificant works" (p. 367). And the priority is unambiguous: "the automatization of cognitive processes, at least within the field of librarianship and publishing, is a more urgent task" (p. 367).</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-36-<p>The chapter's second half turns to language, where two forecasts must be kept apart. On p. 276 he predicts, without conditions, "a still remote state when it will be easier to control phenomena than to understand all the conditions that enable such control". The sentence after it is conditional: "If we manage to crossbreed algorithms produced by finite machines with nonalgorithmic streams of information emerging from phenomena supervised by self-organizing gradients, causal language will stop being comprehending, while science will be producing predictions stripped of 'explanations'" (p. 277). On p. 280, stronger again: natural language, using "tools that have become autonomized on an informational level through being located in extracerebral material systems," will develop a causal language "of the next kind" crossing "the threshold of 'understanding'" — causal in his strict sense, the language of heredity, where "word literally becomes flesh" (p. 273). The last two sections he labels speculation and this book takes them at that value: "The Engineering of Transcendence" builds an afterlife by machine and argues itself out of it — "to prove belief is to destroy it" — closing socially: "It is not a renaissance of transcendence that is needed but rather a renaissance of society" (p. 286). "Cosmogonic Engineering" designs worlds whose inhabitants must not find the exit (pp. 289–290); essay 6 scored the inhabitants' side. Baseline — mid-1960s, revised for the 1974 edition: Ashby's <em>Design for a Brain</em>, Amarel's 1962 paper cited in his own note 1, and lab automation that measured — the AutoAnalyzer of 1957, the "Robot Chemist" of 1959 — but formed no hypotheses. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch7 --></p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-37-</section>
