chapters/07-information-farming.html-67-<p>He required two couplings where the design of automated discovery usually gets one. The sieve must be wired to the phenomenon — every case in this essay where automation held up obeyed that rule, and every contested one substituted a proxy — and the apparatus must stay wired to an addressee: "all information assumes the existence of an addressee. There is no 'information in general'" (p. 243). Note 8 states the failure condition precisely: "As long as the selectors at the information farm are under the active influence of intelligent beings, they are capable of selecting information effectively" (p. 366). He drew the practical conclusion too — a farm "will be particularly useful if it is presented with specific tasks, although outlined in a general manner" (p. 366) — the configuration that works in the systems surveyed here. And he ranked the jobs: addressing is "a more urgent task" (p. 367) than production.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-68-</section>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-69-
chapters/07-information-farming.html-70-<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
chapters/07-information-farming.html:71:<p>The single wrong call is the machine librarian. He looked straight at citation counting in 1964, called algorithmic selection worthless, and it became the standard apparatus for finding scientific work — then, extended to hyperlinks, the ranking method behind web search. The error has a shape worth naming: he assumed selection on relevance required comprehension. The cheap statistical proxy he refused was good enough, and he missed it because he was looking for a sieve wired to a phenomenon, while the citation graph is wired to an addressee — to what other scientists had already found worth using.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-72-<p>The substrate bet was the expensive part. "We need to represent processes with some other processes, not with formal signs" (p. 263) is the load-bearing premise of the design, and it forced a carrier that had to be invented before anything could be tried — he half-admits it: "we do not know how to build an 'information carrier' or where to look for the building material" (p. 266). The premise was too strong. He set the choice up as written formalism against developing chemistry, and what worked was neither: a formalism that executes. His quadrillions-of-formulae argument is about writing things down, and stops applying once the signs are made to run.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-73-<p>And neither half asks what happens when running the phenomenon is itself the expensive step, so that a cheap proxy replaces an expensive world by economics rather than carelessness — that the cheap-to-evaluate problems and the important ones may be different sets is this essay's framing, not his.</p>
chapters/07-information-farming.html-74-
chapters/07-information-farming.html-75-<section class="open">
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-30-<p>The chapter opens with a question, not a gadget: "how do we create realities for the intelligent beings that exist in them, realities that are absolutely indistinguishable from the standard reality but that are subject to different laws?" (p. 197). The modest version — an artificial reality "very similar to the actual one yet that cannot be distinguished from it in any way" — is what he names <em>phantomatics</em>. He builds toward it through a crude thought experiment — record every nerve impulse of a man smelling a rose, play the tape back into him in a dark bath — and pauses on a detail that will matter for sixty years: film has "a predetermined focus," so "we cannot shift our gaze from the foreground, which is in focus, to the background," and a perfect imitation "must provide the eye with freedom also with regard to accommodation" (pp. 198–199).</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-31-<p>Then he throws the tape away. Playback is one-way, and one-way is what art already is: "Art is a unidirectional information transfer. We are just its addressees" (p. 200). The definition to hold on to: "Phantomatics stands for creating bidirectional links between the 'artificial reality' and its recipient. In other words, phantomatics is a feedback art" (p. 200), and it "stands for creating situations in which there are no 'exits' from the worlds of created fiction into the real world" (p. 201). The machine has to react: when the man turns to look behind the pharaoh's throne, "the impulses sent to his neck muscles by his brain must be immediately 'countered' so that the afferent projection of the optical image changes" (p. 202). And it has to populate the vision with partners who have "a (relative) autonomy of speech and action from the actions and words of the protagonist. They must not be mere puppets" (pp. 202–203). "It is easier to imitate a Miss World than Einstein. In the latter case, the machine would have to have the complexity, or intelligence, that would match the brain of a genius at its disposal" (p. 203). He is candid that none of it exists: "for the time being, neither a phantomatic machine nor any programs for it exist" (p. 202).</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-32-<p>The heart of the chapter is the detection problem: can a person "in full possession of his mental faculties" tell they are inside? He gives the tests in ascending order. Do a hundred squats — "if we get sweaty, if we feel a little out of breath, if our heart is pounding and our muscles feel tired, we are in the real world" — until the machine learns to raise the carbon dioxide you breathe (pp. 205–206). Then "'Playing intellectual games with the machine' is the only thing left" — go and talk to a philosopher you know. Here he ranks two machines by difficulty, and the ranking has to be read exactly: "a machine that imitates an interlocutor endowed with intelligence must be much more complex than a machine that creates 'soap opera' kinds of scenes such as the landing of a saucer full of Martians. As a matter of fact, a 'travel' phantomat and a 'human-producing' one are two different kinds of devices. It is incomparably more difficult to construct the second one than the first" (p. 206). Both are phantomats — nerve-connected, full-sensorium; the second adds an autonomous mind inside the world. He is ranking cost, not date, and he is pricing by the complexity of the thing faked. The last test is private knowledge — the drawer that sticks: "The easiest way to debunk a vision is through similar details" (p. 206) — and even that fails. So the conclusion is a probability: the statement that one is in the real world "can always be only probable, sometimes highly probable, but never absolutely certain" (p. 207). Since asking a friend for help only feeds the machine, "a phantomatic world is a world of total solitude. There cannot be more than one person in it at any one time" (p. 207). The omniplanetary "Superphantomat" that follows is a reductio — a civilisation wired in for good "would only exist for the duration of one generation" (p. 207) — an argument, not a forecast.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-33-<p>He sorts the field: "peripheral" phantomatics feeds the senses; "central phantomatics," direct stimulation of the brain's reward centres after Olds and Milner's rats, would be "a technology of facilitated ecstasy" that "cannot in any case be considered an art" (p. 210). Then he cuts his claim down: phantomatics "can mainly be a form of entertainment" (p. 214), yet beside training pilots and surgeons, keeping star-ship crews sane, and sight for the blind "except for those who suffer from total blindness, i.e., whose primary visual cortex has been damaged," "its entertainment role may turn out to be rather marginal" (p. 215). Two limits matter below. The phantomat "cannot be used to determine attitudes, opinions, beliefs, or feelings directly" (p. 213) — but prolonged staged exposure is another matter, and "peripheral phantomatics can also, through appropriate maneuvers, lead to a change of opinion, even of a deep-rooted one. The more life experience a person has accumulated, the more difficult such a change is to accomplish" (p. 217).</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:34:<p>The second half leaves the phantomat. <em>Cerebromatics</em> is the direct rewrite: "Phantomatics provides the brain with 'false' information; cerebromatics falsifies, that is, 'transforms,' the brain itself" (p. 216). Its reach is narrow — you can lobotomise, disinhibit, regress, but "it is impossible to add, in the proper sense of this term, absent traits to one's psyche" through such maneuvers (p. 216), and writing a skill in directly is "not simple even on a theoretical level" (p. 217); to turn Mr. Smith into Napoleon would produce "a completely new personality" so different "that our procedure will practically have to be described as murder" (p. 219). Hence "one can only be oneself or nobody" (p. 221). <em>Teletaxy</em> is the proxy body — "exact models of the human" whose receptors and motor nerves are wired to yours, so a "remote-I" can stand "inside the crater of a volcano" while you sit in Warsaw, limited by signal delay: on the Moon, "significantly delayed reactions" (p. 222). Then "Personality and Information": Wiener's "'telegraphing' man" (p. 225), an atomic description sent by wire and rebuilt. Broadcast it and Smith emerges "a million times over" (p. 226); but the copy "is not the original, as it has a different history" (p. 229), so "telegraphing a person remains out of the question" (p. 229) — killing the original to complete the trip is murder with the evidence tidied away. His clinching move is two scanners, one that leaves the original alive and one that vaporises it: since the copies are identical, "the original's fate is of no consequence for what happens at the other end of the wire" (p. 235). On the way: "We can expect that such a freezing technology, promising as it does 100 percent chance of a future revival at any given moment, will be accomplished" (p. 231). Baseline — mid-1960s, revised for the 1974 edition this translation follows: Heilig's Sensorama and his 1960 patent for a head-worn stereoscopic "Telesphere Mask," Philco's head-tracked Headsight of 1961, stereo film and the circarama; split-brain monkeys; Olds and Milner's rats. <!-- CHECK: telesphere-1960 --> <!-- CHECK: headsight-1961 --> Later, as Zylinska summarises the 1982 and 1991 afterwords, he pointed to "virtual reality devices such as Eye Phone and Data Glove" as evidence of what he "did manage to predict correctly" (p. 21). The afterwords are not in this edition; the ledger scores that self-grade only as far as her summary carries it.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-35-</section>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-36-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-37-<section class="happened">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-38-<h2>What happened</h2>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:39:<p>The travel phantomat's cheap version came slowly and stalled. Sutherland's head-mounted display of 1968 tracked the head from outside and redrew the view — Lem's p. 202 function, by another mechanism. <!-- CHECK: sutherland-1968 --> The Oculus Rift Kickstarter of 2012 was bought by Facebook two years later. <!-- CHECK: oculus-2012 --> The industry's own tracker put combined AR/VR shipments at a peak of about 11 million units in 2021; in 2025 the category grew again only because smart glasses did — VR and mixed-reality headsets themselves fell. <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --> Against a billion-plus smartphones a year, a rounding error. <!-- CHECK: smartphone-scale --> Meta's Reality Labs has lost more than 80 billion dollars since 2020. <!-- CHECK: reality-labs-losses --> The problem Lem named on pp. 198–199 — a fixed focal plane — has a descendant with a name, the vergence–accommodation conflict, still present in shipping headsets; varifocal prototypes have existed since 2018 and none has shipped. <!-- CHECK: vac-unsolved --> <!-- CHECK: varifocal-prototypes --> Measured against his definition — bidirectional, no exits, indistinguishable — nothing on sale is a phantomat, which is why the later Lem's pointing at EyePhone and DataGlove reads, from here, as premature.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-40-<p>The detection game got played anyway — and the transposing here is the essay's, not Lem's. Two years after <em>Summa</em>, Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA, a short pattern-matching script playing a therapist, was confided in by people who knew what it was, to its author's alarm. <!-- CHECK: eliza-1966 --> In 2025 a pre-registered three-party Turing test — a judge talking to a human and a model for five minutes, then choosing — found GPT-4.5, when told to play a persona, judged to be the human 73 percent of the time. <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --> Five minutes of text is not Lem's "human-producing" phantomat, which speaks to your nerves inside a faked world and must survive a long game with a clever opponent. What the text systems show is narrower and still striking: <em>on the conversational channel</em>, a judge who knows one of two partners is a machine, and has five minutes to pick, picks wrong more often than not. Hundreds of millions talk to such a system weekly; <!-- CHECK: chatgpt-scale --> a small minority as a companion — OpenAI's own study finds emotional engagement rare — and companion apps sell that use. <!-- CHECK: openai-affective-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: companion-apps --> The neurotics he predicted for the phantomat's waiting room have been <em>reported</em> in the chatbot's — 2025 newspaper accounts of people whose sense of what was real came apart inside long conversations with a model; reports are what they are. <!-- CHECK: ai-psychosis-2025 --> The other cheap fake is the one he classed as art: the one-way image. Generated images made "can you tell?" a mass problem on exactly the channel he set outside phantomatics — in May 2023 a fake photograph of an explosion at the Pentagon spread on social media and briefly moved the stock market. <!-- CHECK: pentagon-fake-2023 --></p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-41-<p>The second half of the chapter has a 2026 side too. Cerebromatics, writing to the brain, is where brain–computer interfaces are weakest: the implants in human trials read motor intent out — Neuralink's first participant, in 2024, moved a cursor <!-- CHECK: neuralink-2024 --> — and the write side is stimulation: sensation restored by intracortical microstimulation, mood and movement modulated by deep-brain electrodes; nothing yet that adds a skill. <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --> His blessing for the blind whose cortex is intact exists as a six-person feasibility trial of a cortical visual prosthesis. <!-- CHECK: orion-cortical --> His remote-I has both halves in trials — a paralysed woman drove a robotic arm from her motor cortex in 2012; tactile feedback was written into a participant's cortex in 2021 <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --> — while teleoperation without the nerve link is routine: a New York surgeon removed a gallbladder in Strasbourg in 2001 at a delay of about 155 milliseconds; his lunar second-each-way would still disqualify a surgeon. <!-- CHECK: lindbergh-2001 --> His reversible freezing has not been accomplished for any mammal — vitrified rat kidneys were rewarmed and transplanted with lasting function in 2023; there is no dated path from an organ to an organism, and no human has been revived — while cryonics has been selling the promise since 1972. <!-- CHECK: kidney-vitrification-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: alcor-1972 --> His telegraph paradox has the structure of the cases Parfit made canonical in <em>Reasons and Persons</em> (1984) — the teletransporter, the branch line — and the upload debate runs on them still. <!-- CHECK: parfit-1984 --> His "only probable, never certain" (p. 207) is where the skeptical literature still sits; Bostrom's simulation argument (2003) is a cousin, not the same result. <!-- CHECK: bostrom-2003 --> <!-- CHECK: chalmers-2022 --></p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-42-</section>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-43-
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-54-<tr><td>Phantomatics "can mainly be a form of entertainment" (p. 214) — yet "its entertainment role may turn out to be rather marginal" (p. 215)</td><td>Hedged both ways about an unbuilt machine; the headsets that shipped are games-led, but they are not it <!-- CHECK: vr-use-mix --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-55-<tr><td>"a true blessing for the blind" whose "primary visual cortex" is intact (p. 215)</td><td>Cortical visual prosthesis in a six-participant early-feasibility trial <!-- CHECK: orion-cortical --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-56-<tr><td>Teletaxy: a "remote-I" whose receptors and motor nerves are wired to yours (p. 222)</td><td>Motor read-out drives a robotic arm (2012); tactile feedback written into cortex (2021); both in trials, neither combined into a proxy body <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-57-<tr><td>Cerebromatics: "impossible to add … absent traits" through such maneuvers (p. 216); a direct skill write "not simple even on a theoretical level" (p. 217)</td><td>Neural write in humans is stimulation and modulation; the impossibility is untested, not confirmed <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:58:<tr><td>"We can expect that such a freezing technology, promising … 100 percent chance of a future revival … will be accomplished" (p. 231)</td><td>Organ-scale vitrification in rats, 2023; no whole mammal revived and no dated path from organ to organism <!-- CHECK: kidney-vitrification-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: alcor-1972 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-59-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-60-<tr><td>The cost of a fake scales with the complexity of the thing faked — Miss World cheaper than Einstein, interlocutor dearer than saucer (pp. 203, 206)</td><td>This essay's transposition: the fakes that fool people in 2026 are the cheapest ones — a warned judge loses a five-minute text test; the scarce resource was the receiver's credulity, not the simulator's complexity <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: eliza-1966 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-61-<tr><td>Indistinguishability belongs to feedback systems; one-way transfer is art (p. 200)</td><td>The mass "can you tell?" problem arrived in one-way generated images — the 2023 Pentagon fake <!-- CHECK: pentagon-fake-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-62-<tr><td>That one is in the real world "can always be only probable … but never absolutely certain" (p. 207)</td><td>The live skeptical literature holds the same (Chalmers 2022); Bostrom's 2003 argument is a related, different result <!-- CHECK: chalmers-2022 --> <!-- CHECK: bostrom-2003 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
--
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-22-<main class="wrap">
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-23-
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-24-<p class="kicker">Essay 2 · Lem ch. 2, "Two Evolutions" (2013 tr., pp. 31–58)</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-25-<h1>The last lawnmower</h1>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:26:<p class="lede">Lem spends five pages describing the life cycle of a species — arriving small and primitive, copying its predecessor, radiating into niches, swelling into giants before the end, leaving relics in the margins — and then tells you he has been describing two things at once. It is the best sustained analogy in the book. He also, more cautiously than he is usually given credit for, offers a scenario in which the car is replaced by an air-cushion vehicle and a small petrol lawnmower survives as its last descendant. The air-cushion vehicle did not happen. What did happen to the lawnmower is something his model has no term for.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-27-
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-28-<section class="said">
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-29-<h2>What Lem said</h2>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-30-<p>The chapter opens on a question about direction: "Who causes whom? Does technology cause us, or do we cause it?" (p. 32). To answer it he borrows de Latil's ladder of effectors and puts technical evolution near the top, as a system with "freedom of choice with regard to its building material" (p. 33).</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-31-<p>Then the set piece. He describes, without saying of what, the arc of a species: "A new species appears in the world unnoticed. Its appearance seems to come from what already exists, and this borrowing seems to testify to the inventive inertia of the Designer" (p. 33). The first specimens are small, carrying "a number of primitive features, as if their birth had been hurried" (p. 34); then an environmental shift lets them expand, and in empty space the lineage "bursts into evolutionary radiation" (p. 34). Decline has a signature: "the specimens are getting bigger, as if gigantism was to provide protection against the threat" (p. 34), while lateral branches persist as "the last proof of the primeval abundance and power of a species" (p. 34). Only then does he admit the trick: "I have been talking about two kinds of evolution at the same time: biological and technical" (p. 34).</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-32-<p>The examples are specific. "The first automobile was a spitting image of the coach with a guillotined shaft; the airplane had been 'copied' from the kite (or even the bird), the radio from the already existing telephone" (p. 35). Prototypes are poor, just as "The feathered lizard—the archaeopteryx—did not as much fly as flutter" (p. 35). Then radiation: the car "'gave birth' to the bus, the truck, the bulldozer, the fire engine, the tank" (p. 35). Then a mechanism, stated in the present tense about a machine that already existed: "just as the predator's strategy affects the strategy of its prey, so the classical airplane defends itself against the helicopter's invasion. It does this by creating a prototype of the winged plane, which—owing to the change of direction of the thrust—is capable of taking off and landing vertically" (p. 35). And the gigantism signature: "The last zeppelins of the 1930s can be easily compared with the atlantosauruses and brontosauruses of the Cretaceous period" (pp. 35–36).</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:33:<p>Then the passage this essay is named for, with its hedges intact. "One can also imagine further development of mechanical vehicles, which will make wheel drive obsolete. When the current automobile is eventually replaced by an 'air-cushion vehicle,' it is quite likely that, say, a small combustion-driven lawnmower will be the last descendant of the classical car—still vegetating in a 'lateral branch'" (p. 36). It is a scenario, not a dated forecast: he says "one can also imagine", and "it is quite likely that, say".</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-34-<p>The claim underneath the analogy is stated once: both are "material processes with almost the same number of degrees of freedom and with similar dynamic laws" (p. 38). He is not claiming that technology runs itself. The same page says technical evolution "is not, for the time being at least, people-free", and that "It is here perhaps that the biggest difference lies: bioevolution is beyond all doubt an amoral process, which is something we cannot say about technical evolution" (p. 38).</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:35:<p>Two further claims belong to this essay. On universality: "the universality of our technologies is minimal. Technical evolution has so far been moving in a kind of reverse direction to biological evolution in that it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices" (p. 43), and the way out is named — "The potential for universality seems to lie in a further development of the theory of self-organizing systems—which would be capable of adaptive self-programming" (pp. 43–44). And in "Several Naïve Questions" he refuses the consolation that creative work is safe: "Apparently it will only be possible to automatize noncreative mental labor. But where is the evidence? Let us be clear about it: there is none, and what is more, there can be none" (p. 56). The displacement "will not happen in the narrow sense of laborers and technicians being replaced by programmers of digital machines because future generations and future types of these machines will not need programmers anymore" (p. 57). And he separates capability from motive: "'cleverer' does not yet mean 'rebellious'" (p. 57).</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-36-<p>Note 6 (pp. 355–357), which hangs not from the parallel but from the end of the theory-versus-empiricism discussion at p. 47, qualifies the analogy where the chapter's own "Differences" section does not. "The difficulty is that the current increase in knowledge is caused by interspecies hybridization in technology but not in biology. Any randomly chosen areas of human activity are capable of cross-fertilization… yet biological species, having become fixed, cannot reproduce through crossbreeding." Hence technoevolution shows "constant acceleration", its forecasts are hard "owing to the occurrence of sudden turns—which are totally unexpected and unpredictable", and "The number of 'technological species' emerging at any given time is a function of all the existing species, which is something we cannot say about bioevolution."</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-37-<p>The baseline needs a caveat this book applies elsewhere: Zylinska translates the altered fourth edition of 1974, so the text's state of the art is mid-1970s, not 1964. That matters here. The Harrier entered service in 1969 and the car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft in 1968 — both before this edition — so neither can be treated as something the text predicted. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch2 --></p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-38-</section>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-39-
--
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-43-<p>The relic half is being decided by something outside the model. Lem's small petrol engine is supposed to outlive the car in a lateral branch. Instead California required most newly manufactured small off-road engines — petrol engines at or below 25 horsepower, which covers lawnmowers — to be zero-emission from January 2024, while existing machines stay legal to own and resell. <!-- CHECK: sore-ban-2024 --> The relic is not being outcompeted; the niche is being closed to new entrants by regulation. Lem is clear that technical evolution is "not… people-free" and, unlike bioevolution, not amoral (p. 38) — but the chapter models human agency as choice about direction, not as a body that can prohibit a fit technology and protect an unfit one.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-44-<p>The same pressure runs the other way too. Manufacturers began dropping AM radio from electric cars around 2023, because the high-voltage drivetrain interferes with reception — habitat loss, and the reverse of the diversification under television pressure Lem describes at p. 36. What followed has no biological counterpart: the older technology acquired a lobby. The AM Radio for Every Vehicle Act, reintroduced in January 2025 and advanced by the Senate Commerce Committee within a week, would compel manufacturers to carry AM reception from model year 2028. <!-- CHECK: am-radio-ev --> Species do not petition a legislature against their competitors.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-45-<p>Where the analogy earns its keep is the mechanism, not the artifacts. The vertically launching aeroplane already existed when he wrote — the P.1127 hovered in 1960, the Harrier entered service in 1969 — so the forecast is not that it would appear. The testable part is his account of <em>why</em>: a lineage defending a niche rather than conquering one. The V-22 entered service in 2007 and the F-35B in 2015, and neither displaced the helicopter, which is what niche defence predicts and displacement does not. <!-- CHECK: vtol-aircraft --> The civil version, which he did not describe, remains uncertified. <!-- CHECK: evtol-2026 --></p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-46-<p>The pattern holds where machines compete for a physical niche, and something else happens where they do not. Software relics do not retreat to the margins; they stay in the middle. COBOL, written for mainframes contemporary with this book, is still load-bearing in core banking, on industry estimates that put it somewhere between roughly 220 and 800 billion lines in production — figures that are trade estimates rather than audited counts. <!-- CHECK: cobol-production --> The direction is not in doubt even if the number is: this is not a lateral branch vegetating in a peripheral niche, it is the centre of the financial system sixty years after the environment that produced it disappeared. In software the cost of replacement falls on whoever switches, and nothing forces the switch, so a relic can occupy the core indefinitely.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:47:<p>His p. 43 claim went the other way. When he wrote it, technology had "only been creating narrowly specialized devices"; the direction then reversed, and the systems that now matter most are valued for not being specialised. He named the route — universality via "self-organizing systems… capable of adaptive self-programming" — and what arrived is self-programmed in a loose sense, learned rather than written, but by gradient descent on large corpora rather than the theory he meant. <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> His refusal at p. 56 to reserve creative work for people has held: what automated first was writing, drawing and coding. Programmers have not been dispensed with. <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --></p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-48-<p>Note 6's mechanism — cross-fertilisation between technical domains, which biology cannot perform — is visible in the origin of that technology. The founding result of deep learning was trained on two consumer graphics cards built for rendering video games. <!-- CHECK: alexnet-2012 --> That is one instance of cross-domain recombination, offered as this essay's illustration rather than as proof of the note's larger claims about acceleration or unpredictability, which it cannot carry alone.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-49-</section>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-50-
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-51-<h2>The ledger</h2>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-52-<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-53-<thead><tr><th>Lem (page)</th><th>2026 (receipt)</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-54-<tbody>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-55-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Object level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:56:<tr><td>"One can also imagine further development of mechanical vehicles, which will make wheel drive obsolete. When the current automobile is eventually replaced by an 'air-cushion vehicle'…" (p. 36)</td><td>Wheel drive is untouched. The car-carrying cross-Channel hovercraft closed in 2000 and large commercial hovercraft effectively disappeared; the combustion engine is being displaced by an electric motor under the same wheels <!-- CHECK: hovercraft-2000 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-57-<tr><td>"it is quite likely that, say, a small combustion-driven lawnmower will be the last descendant of the classical car—still vegetating in a 'lateral branch'" (p. 36)</td><td>Undecided, and being decided elsewhere: new small petrol engines could no longer be manufactured for sale in California from 2024, though existing ones stay legal, while at least three-quarters of new cars still burn fuel <!-- CHECK: sore-ban-2024 --> <!-- CHECK: ev-share-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:58:<tr><td>Technical evolution "has so far been moving in a kind of reverse direction to biological evolution in that it has only been creating narrowly specialized devices" (p. 43)</td><td>The direction reversed. The systems that now matter most are valued for being general rather than specialised <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:59:<tr><td>"The potential for universality seems to lie in a further development of the theory of self-organizing systems—which would be capable of adaptive self-programming" (pp. 43–44)</td><td>Universality did come from systems that program themselves rather than being written — but by gradient descent on large corpora, not from the theory of self-organizing systems he meant <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --> <!-- CHECK: scaling-laws-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-ra">RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-60-<tr><td>On the claim that only "noncreative mental labor" can be automated: "where is the evidence? Let us be clear about it: there is none, and what is more, there can be none" (p. 56)</td><td>He was right to refuse it. What automated first was writing, drawing and coding, not the manual residue <!-- CHECK: gpt3-2020 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-61-<tr><td>Displacement "will not happen in the narrow sense of laborers and technicians being replaced by programmers… because future generations and future types of these machines will not need programmers anymore" (p. 57)</td><td>Half arrived: models now write and revise programs inside automated loops, but the systems are still built and directed by programmers <!-- CHECK: funsearch-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: ai-scientist-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-62-<tr><td>The mechanism behind vertical take-off: "the classical airplane defends itself against the helicopter's invasion" (p. 35) — niche defence, not conquest</td><td>The aircraft already existed when he wrote, so only the mechanism is testable. It holds after the text: the V-22 entered service in 2007 and the F-35B in 2015, and neither displaced the helicopter <!-- CHECK: vtol-aircraft --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-63-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
--
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-71-<p>He treated the analogy as a claim with consequences rather than a figure of speech, and he named mechanisms — niche defence, radiation into empty space, relics in lateral branches — instead of gesturing at resemblance. Sixty years of technology writing has since made "ecosystem", "DNA" and "evolution" into decoration, applied to firms and products with nothing at stake and nothing checkable. His version can be graded, which is why there is a MISS in this ledger at all. He was also better than most of his successors on two specific points. He refused, in 1966, the consolation that creative work is safe from automation, and refused it on the right ground: not that machines would soon write poems, but that nobody had any evidence for the exemption and no such evidence could exist. And in the same paragraph he kept capability apart from motive — "'cleverer' does not yet mean 'rebellious'" (p. 57) — which is precisely the inference most current argument about intelligent machines runs together.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-72-</section>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-73-
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-74-<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html:75:<p>He picked the wrong successor for the car, and picked it for a reason worth naming. The air-cushion vehicle was, when he wrote, the impressive machine: it did the thing that looked hard, abolishing the wheel. What actually displaced the combustion engine was a substitution so conservative it kept the wheels, the roads, the shape and the name. His own framework handles that better than his example did — evolution changes what is cheap to change, and a lineage under pressure does not redesign its body plan — but the framework is in the chapter and the hovercraft is in the prediction.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-76-<p>The larger gap is a pressure the model does not contain. Lem is explicit that technical evolution is not people-free and not amoral, so this is not a chapter that thinks technology runs itself. But the agency he models is directional: societies choosing what to build and how fast. What decided the fate of the small petrol engine was neither competition nor choice of direction but a rule about what may be manufactured, and what is keeping AM radio alive in cars is a bill. A regulator can close a niche to a technology that is winning it and compel the carriage of one that is losing. Between "competition" and "human choice" there is a third thing — enforceable prohibition — and the two-evolutions frame has no slot for it, which is why the chapter's most concrete illustration is being settled in a way the chapter cannot describe.</p>
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-77-
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-78-<section class="open">
chapters/02-the-last-lawnmower.html-79-<h2>Still open</h2>
--
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-3-<head>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-4-<meta charset="utf-8">
