chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-22-<main class="wrap">
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-23-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-24-<p class="kicker">Essay 3 · Lem ch. 3, "Civilizations in the Universe" (2013 tr., pp. 59–92)</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-25-<h1>Who pays for the telescope</h1>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:26:<p class="lede">Thirty pages of this chapter ask why nobody answers, and the answer Lem settles on is not in the chapter at all — it is in note 3, where he says which hypothesis he has "selected as the dominant" one. The last two pages then ask a different kind of question: what a search would cost, who would pay for it, who would be willing to transmit, and whether we could recognise a message if it arrived. Those two pages have aged better than the thirty, and they explain something the thirty cannot: after sixty years of listening, we have searched about a drinking glass of the ocean.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-27-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-28-<section class="said">
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-29-<h2>What Lem said</h2>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-30-<p>The problem is stated with numbers. Drake's instruments at Green Bank had listened since 1960, and "Day after day, week after week, their instruments registered nothing but the monotonous cosmic noise generated by dead matter" (p. 64). Bracewell and von Hoerner had computed civilisation density "from the premise that only 1 out of 150 stars in our Galaxy has planets" (p. 63) — Lem calls it modest — which still gives a billion planetary systems, and if civilisations last as long as their suns then "the average distance between two civilizations would be less than ten light-years" (pp. 63–64). Since nothing is heard, the estimates for civilisation lifetime were ratcheted down until they fit the silence: to a hundred million years, then to "less than twenty thousand years" (p. 65), a thousand light-years apart. Lem's question, put twice: "Why, in other words, are we seeing no 'miracles'?" (p. 67).</p>
--
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-37-</section>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-38-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-39-<section class="happened">
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-40-<h2>What happened</h2>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:41:<p>Start with how much listening has been done, because every other verdict depends on it. In 2018 Wright, Kanodia and Lubar built an eight-dimensional model of the radio search space — sky position, frequency, sensitivity, polarisation, drift, duration and the rest — and computed the fraction that decades of programmes had collectively covered. It is minute: comparable to examining a drinking glass of seawater for fish in all of Earth's oceans, and a later survey using the same framework reported a search fraction around two parts in ten thousand trillion. <!-- CHECK: seti-haystack-2018 --> Sixty years of silence is a real observation about a very small volume.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:42:<p>The money went as he feared, though not in the shape he described. NASA's High Resolution Microwave Survey — the last major federally funded American search — began observing in October 1992 at about $10 million a year, under 0.1 percent of NASA's budget, and was killed about a year later by a last-minute Senate amendment. <!-- CHECK: nasa-seti-1993 --> After that, dedicated American radio SETI leaned on philanthropy: roughly $30 million from Paul Allen for the array that carries his name, which went into hibernation in April 2011 when federal and state funding collapsed and returned that December partly by renting itself to the Air Force to track orbital debris; <!-- CHECK: ata-2011 --> then $100 million from a technology billionaire in 2015; <!-- CHECK: breakthrough-listen-2015 --> then, in 2023, a $200 million bequest from the estate of a Qualcomm co-founder, against an operating budget of $25–30 million a year. <!-- CHECK: seti-bequest-2023 --> But the instruments themselves stayed public. Breakthrough Listen does its observing on the NSF-funded Green Bank Telescope and CSIRO's Parkes dish, not on anything it built. <!-- CHECK: bl-public-facilities --> The rulers did not fund the search; they had already funded the telescopes.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-43-<p>That also complicates his price. He asked for "a great amount of equipment" and possibly transmitters, and compared the bill to nuclear-energy research; what happened was a programme commitment of about $10 million a year riding on public infrastructure, against $790 million for US fusion in a single year and more than $20 billion sunk into ITER. <!-- CHECK: fusion-spending --> <!-- CHECK: breakthrough-listen-2015 --> Those are not like-for-like quantities, and nobody has assembled the all-in figure his sentence actually names. What can be said is that no state ever treated the search as a project of that size, and that the searching which did happen was cheap because it borrowed dishes built for something else.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-44-<p>The transmission asymmetry is real and remains the field's live dispute, though the history is less tidy than "nobody transmits". Frank Drake broadcast the Arecibo message toward M13 in 1974, with considerable fanfare; <!-- CHECK: arecibo-1974 --> a Russian astronomer sent several messages from Evpatoria; and METI International was founded in 2015 to send more. What is true is that sustained deliberate transmission never became normal, and that in the same year a statement issued around the AAAS meeting held that any decision to transmit "must be based upon a worldwide consensus, and not a decision based upon the wishes of a few individuals with access to powerful communications equipment." <!-- CHECK: meti-2015 --> Everyone listens and almost nobody calls, which is Lem's economic trap with a moral argument now bolted onto it.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:45:<p>His information argument is sound and its novelty is smaller than it looks: Ozma was already hunting artificial regularity against galactic noise when he wrote, as he says himself at p. 64. What the argument explains is why the method stayed that way. Radio SETI still concentrates on narrowband, repetitive signals, because natural astrophysical emission is broadband while an efficiently encoded transmission is statistically hard to distinguish from noise — so the detectable beacon is by construction the one somebody wasted bandwidth to make detectable. <!-- CHECK: narrowband-seti --> His stronger claim, that the conversations may already be in the archives as background, remains untestable and is a live reason the null result is weaker than it sounds.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-46-<p>Star flight has not moved, but sixty years is not the several hundred he allowed. No propulsion has appeared that changes von Hoerner's arithmetic. The only funded interstellar programme abandoned the crewed ship entirely: Breakthrough Starshot, announced in 2016 with $100 million, proposes gram-scale laser-driven sails to a neighbouring star — a different journey from the intergalactic crossing von Hoerner costed, and a proof-of-concept programme rather than a mission. <!-- CHECK: starshot-2016 --> It is privately funded too.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-47-</section>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-48-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-49-<h2>The ledger</h2>
--
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-59-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-60-<tr><td>Contact is a problem of cost and incentive — who builds, who pays, who transmits — not only of physics and biology (pp. 89–90)</td><td>The shape of the field. Its history is a funding history: a programme cancelled by amendment, an array saved by tracking orbital debris, a search bought privately and run on public dishes, an endowment left in a will <!-- CHECK: ata-2011 --> <!-- CHECK: bl-public-facilities --> <!-- CHECK: seti-bequest-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-61-<tr><td>"If, for economic reasons, all the civilizations were only working on reception, then no one would ever hear anyone else, as many have already pointed out" (p. 90) — credited by him to others</td><td>Holds as a recurring incentive problem, on the only case observable: Earth listens continuously and has transmitted deliberately a handful of times since Arecibo in 1974, with a 2015 statement demanding worldwide consensus before anyone transmits again <!-- CHECK: arecibo-1974 --> <!-- CHECK: meti-2015 --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-62-<tr><td>An efficiently coded message "starts to resemble noise", so a civilisation wanting to be found must send simple, repeatable "call signs" (p. 90)</td><td>Correct, and it explains why the method he watched being born stayed put: searches still hunt narrowband repetitive beacons because natural emission is broadband and dense encoding is indistinguishable from noise. Ozma was already doing this in 1960, so the argument explains a practice rather than predicting one <!-- CHECK: narrowband-seti --></td><td class="verdict v-hit">HIT</td></tr>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:63:<tr><td>"a complete absence of any positive data will be of even greater significance—which will increase even further the longer such attempts take and the more sensitive the instruments used in them are" (p. 80) — with "Today it is still too early for this"</td><td>Still too early, on his own terms and by a wide margin. The eight-dimensional accounting puts the fraction of the radio search space examined at roughly a drinking glass against Earth's oceans <!-- CHECK: seti-haystack-2018 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-64-</tbody></table></div>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-65-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-66-<section class="better">
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-67-<h2>Where Lem was better than us</h2>
--
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-69-</section>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-70-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-71-<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-72-<p>He costed the wrong object and framed the problem as scale. His estimate — more than nuclear-energy research — set contact up as something only a state could attempt, which made political will the binding constraint. What actually bound was neither money nor will but attention. The sums were small enough that a single donor could cover them and small enough that no government felt obliged to; a programme costing 0.1 percent of NASA's budget is not killed because it is expensive but because it is cheap enough to cut without argument and strange enough to make an easy vote. And the equipment problem dissolved in a way he did not foresee: you do not need to build the great amount of equipment if you can rent time on dishes built for radio astronomy.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:73:<p>The second cost lands on the chapter's main argument, and by his own standard. Thirty pages treat the silence as something the theory of biogenesis must answer to, and he does say, carefully, "Today it is still too early for this" (p. 80). Sixty more years of listening have not changed that, because the listening covered a fraction of the search space best expressed in scientific notation. The silence may yet be the universe's answer. It is not yet evidence of anything, and the chapter's confidence that a sufficiently long null result would force a revision of biogenesis is a promissory note that has not begun to come due.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-74-
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-75-<section class="open">
