chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-44-<p>The transmission asymmetry is real and remains the field's live dispute, though the history is less tidy than "nobody transmits". Frank Drake broadcast the Arecibo message toward M13 in 1974, with considerable fanfare; <!-- CHECK: arecibo-1974 --> a Russian astronomer sent several messages from Evpatoria; and METI International was founded in 2015 to send more. What is true is that sustained deliberate transmission never became normal, and that in the same year a statement issued around the AAAS meeting held that any decision to transmit "must be based upon a worldwide consensus, and not a decision based upon the wishes of a few individuals with access to powerful communications equipment." <!-- CHECK: meti-2015 --> Everyone listens and almost nobody calls, which is Lem's economic trap with a moral argument now bolted onto it.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html:45:<p>His information argument is sound and its novelty is smaller than it looks: Ozma was already hunting artificial regularity against galactic noise when he wrote, as he says himself at p. 64. What the argument explains is why the method stayed that way. Radio SETI still concentrates on narrowband, repetitive signals, because natural astrophysical emission is broadband while an efficiently encoded transmission is statistically hard to distinguish from noise — so the detectable beacon is by construction the one somebody wasted bandwidth to make detectable. <!-- CHECK: narrowband-seti --> His stronger claim, that the conversations may already be in the archives as background, remains untestable and is a live reason the null result is weaker than it sounds.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-46-<p>Star flight has not moved, but sixty years is not the several hundred he allowed. No propulsion has appeared that changes von Hoerner's arithmetic. The only funded interstellar programme abandoned the crewed ship entirely: Breakthrough Starshot, announced in 2016 with $100 million, proposes gram-scale laser-driven sails to a neighbouring star — a different journey from the intergalactic crossing von Hoerner costed, and a proof-of-concept programme rather than a mission. <!-- CHECK: starshot-2016 --> It is privately funded too.</p>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-47-</section>
chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html-48-
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-4-<meta charset="utf-8">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-5-<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-6-<title>6 · The cheap channel — Sixty Years Later</title>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:7:<meta name="description" content="Lem's phantomat — a machine you cannot tell from the world — has not been built, and the headsets that stand in for it stalled. The detection game he built it to describe is being played anyway, in 2026, on a channel he never priced: conversation. And the second half of his chapter anticipates the copying-and-identity cases that Parfit later made canonical.">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-8-<link rel="stylesheet" href="../static/style.css">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-9-<script src="../static/theme.js"></script>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-10-</head>
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-23-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-24-<p class="kicker">Essay 6 · Lem ch. 6, "Phantomology" (2013 tr., pp. 197–236)</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-25-<h1>The cheap channel</h1>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:26:<p class="lede">You have probably worn a headset once and taken it off after twenty minutes. You have almost certainly talked to a machine this year that talked back well enough that you forgot, for a sentence, what it was. Neither is the device Lem describes in this chapter — a machine that feeds your nerves a world you cannot tell from the real one — and he would be the first to say so. But the question he built the machine to ask, <em>can you tell?</em>, is being answered in 2026 on a channel he never priced at all — cheap, one-dimensional conversation — and that transposition is this essay's, not his. The second half of his chapter, which almost nobody reads, is the argument about copying and rewriting people whose cases philosophy later made canonical.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-27-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-28-<section class="said">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-29-<h2>What Lem said</h2>
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-31-<p>Then he throws the tape away. Playback is one-way, and one-way is what art already is: "Art is a unidirectional information transfer. We are just its addressees" (p. 200). The definition to hold on to: "Phantomatics stands for creating bidirectional links between the 'artificial reality' and its recipient. In other words, phantomatics is a feedback art" (p. 200), and it "stands for creating situations in which there are no 'exits' from the worlds of created fiction into the real world" (p. 201). The machine has to react: when the man turns to look behind the pharaoh's throne, "the impulses sent to his neck muscles by his brain must be immediately 'countered' so that the afferent projection of the optical image changes" (p. 202). And it has to populate the vision with partners who have "a (relative) autonomy of speech and action from the actions and words of the protagonist. They must not be mere puppets" (pp. 202–203). "It is easier to imitate a Miss World than Einstein. In the latter case, the machine would have to have the complexity, or intelligence, that would match the brain of a genius at its disposal" (p. 203). He is candid that none of it exists: "for the time being, neither a phantomatic machine nor any programs for it exist" (p. 202).</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-32-<p>The heart of the chapter is the detection problem: can a person "in full possession of his mental faculties" tell they are inside? He gives the tests in ascending order. Do a hundred squats — "if we get sweaty, if we feel a little out of breath, if our heart is pounding and our muscles feel tired, we are in the real world" — until the machine learns to raise the carbon dioxide you breathe (pp. 205–206). Then "'Playing intellectual games with the machine' is the only thing left" — go and talk to a philosopher you know. Here he ranks two machines by difficulty, and the ranking has to be read exactly: "a machine that imitates an interlocutor endowed with intelligence must be much more complex than a machine that creates 'soap opera' kinds of scenes such as the landing of a saucer full of Martians. As a matter of fact, a 'travel' phantomat and a 'human-producing' one are two different kinds of devices. It is incomparably more difficult to construct the second one than the first" (p. 206). Both are phantomats — nerve-connected, full-sensorium; the second adds an autonomous mind inside the world. He is ranking cost, not date, and he is pricing by the complexity of the thing faked. The last test is private knowledge — the drawer that sticks: "The easiest way to debunk a vision is through similar details" (p. 206) — and even that fails. So the conclusion is a probability: the statement that one is in the real world "can always be only probable, sometimes highly probable, but never absolutely certain" (p. 207). Since asking a friend for help only feeds the machine, "a phantomatic world is a world of total solitude. There cannot be more than one person in it at any one time" (p. 207). The omniplanetary "Superphantomat" that follows is a reductio — a civilisation wired in for good "would only exist for the duration of one generation" (p. 207) — an argument, not a forecast.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-33-<p>He sorts the field: "peripheral" phantomatics feeds the senses; "central phantomatics," direct stimulation of the brain's reward centres after Olds and Milner's rats, would be "a technology of facilitated ecstasy" that "cannot in any case be considered an art" (p. 210). Then he cuts his claim down: phantomatics "can mainly be a form of entertainment" (p. 214), yet beside training pilots and surgeons, keeping star-ship crews sane, and sight for the blind "except for those who suffer from total blindness, i.e., whose primary visual cortex has been damaged," "its entertainment role may turn out to be rather marginal" (p. 215). Two limits matter below. The phantomat "cannot be used to determine attitudes, opinions, beliefs, or feelings directly" (p. 213) — but prolonged staged exposure is another matter, and "peripheral phantomatics can also, through appropriate maneuvers, lead to a change of opinion, even of a deep-rooted one. The more life experience a person has accumulated, the more difficult such a change is to accomplish" (p. 217).</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:34:<p>The second half leaves the phantomat. <em>Cerebromatics</em> is the direct rewrite: "Phantomatics provides the brain with 'false' information; cerebromatics falsifies, that is, 'transforms,' the brain itself" (p. 216). Its reach is narrow — you can lobotomise, disinhibit, regress, but "it is impossible to add, in the proper sense of this term, absent traits to one's psyche" through such maneuvers (p. 216), and writing a skill in directly is "not simple even on a theoretical level" (p. 217); to turn Mr. Smith into Napoleon would produce "a completely new personality" so different "that our procedure will practically have to be described as murder" (p. 219). Hence "one can only be oneself or nobody" (p. 221). <em>Teletaxy</em> is the proxy body — "exact models of the human" whose receptors and motor nerves are wired to yours, so a "remote-I" can stand "inside the crater of a volcano" while you sit in Warsaw, limited by signal delay: on the Moon, "significantly delayed reactions" (p. 222). Then "Personality and Information": Wiener's "'telegraphing' man" (p. 225), an atomic description sent by wire and rebuilt. Broadcast it and Smith emerges "a million times over" (p. 226); but the copy "is not the original, as it has a different history" (p. 229), so "telegraphing a person remains out of the question" (p. 229) — killing the original to complete the trip is murder with the evidence tidied away. His clinching move is two scanners, one that leaves the original alive and one that vaporises it: since the copies are identical, "the original's fate is of no consequence for what happens at the other end of the wire" (p. 235). On the way: "We can expect that such a freezing technology, promising as it does 100 percent chance of a future revival at any given moment, will be accomplished" (p. 231). Baseline — mid-1960s, revised for the 1974 edition this translation follows: Heilig's Sensorama and his 1960 patent for a head-worn stereoscopic "Telesphere Mask," Philco's head-tracked Headsight of 1961, stereo film and the circarama; split-brain monkeys; Olds and Milner's rats. <!-- CHECK: telesphere-1960 --> <!-- CHECK: headsight-1961 --> Later, as Zylinska summarises the 1982 and 1991 afterwords, he pointed to "virtual reality devices such as Eye Phone and Data Glove" as evidence of what he "did manage to predict correctly" (p. 21). The afterwords are not in this edition; the ledger scores that self-grade only as far as her summary carries it.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-35-</section>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-36-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-37-<section class="happened">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-38-<h2>What happened</h2>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:39:<p>The travel phantomat's cheap version came slowly and stalled. Sutherland's head-mounted display of 1968 tracked the head from outside and redrew the view — Lem's p. 202 function, by another mechanism. <!-- CHECK: sutherland-1968 --> The Oculus Rift Kickstarter of 2012 was bought by Facebook two years later. <!-- CHECK: oculus-2012 --> The industry's own tracker put combined AR/VR shipments at a peak of about 11 million units in 2021; in 2025 the category grew again only because smart glasses did — VR and mixed-reality headsets themselves fell. <!-- CHECK: idc-vr-peak --> Against a billion-plus smartphones a year, a rounding error. <!-- CHECK: smartphone-scale --> Meta's Reality Labs has lost more than 80 billion dollars since 2020. <!-- CHECK: reality-labs-losses --> The problem Lem named on pp. 198–199 — a fixed focal plane — has a descendant with a name, the vergence–accommodation conflict, still present in shipping headsets; varifocal prototypes have existed since 2018 and none has shipped. <!-- CHECK: vac-unsolved --> <!-- CHECK: varifocal-prototypes --> Measured against his definition — bidirectional, no exits, indistinguishable — nothing on sale is a phantomat, which is why the later Lem's pointing at EyePhone and DataGlove reads, from here, as premature.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-40-<p>The detection game got played anyway — and the transposing here is the essay's, not Lem's. Two years after <em>Summa</em>, Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA, a short pattern-matching script playing a therapist, was confided in by people who knew what it was, to its author's alarm. <!-- CHECK: eliza-1966 --> In 2025 a pre-registered three-party Turing test — a judge talking to a human and a model for five minutes, then choosing — found GPT-4.5, when told to play a persona, judged to be the human 73 percent of the time. <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --> Five minutes of text is not Lem's "human-producing" phantomat, which speaks to your nerves inside a faked world and must survive a long game with a clever opponent. What the text systems show is narrower and still striking: <em>on the conversational channel</em>, a judge who knows one of two partners is a machine, and has five minutes to pick, picks wrong more often than not. Hundreds of millions talk to such a system weekly; <!-- CHECK: chatgpt-scale --> a small minority as a companion — OpenAI's own study finds emotional engagement rare — and companion apps sell that use. <!-- CHECK: openai-affective-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: companion-apps --> The neurotics he predicted for the phantomat's waiting room have been <em>reported</em> in the chatbot's — 2025 newspaper accounts of people whose sense of what was real came apart inside long conversations with a model; reports are what they are. <!-- CHECK: ai-psychosis-2025 --> The other cheap fake is the one he classed as art: the one-way image. Generated images made "can you tell?" a mass problem on exactly the channel he set outside phantomatics — in May 2023 a fake photograph of an explosion at the Pentagon spread on social media and briefly moved the stock market. <!-- CHECK: pentagon-fake-2023 --></p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:41:<p>The second half of the chapter has a 2026 side too. Cerebromatics, writing to the brain, is where brain–computer interfaces are weakest: the implants in human trials read motor intent out — Neuralink's first participant, in 2024, moved a cursor <!-- CHECK: neuralink-2024 --> — and the write side is stimulation: sensation restored by intracortical microstimulation, mood and movement modulated by deep-brain electrodes; nothing yet that adds a skill. <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --> His blessing for the blind whose cortex is intact exists as a six-person feasibility trial of a cortical visual prosthesis. <!-- CHECK: orion-cortical --> His remote-I has both halves in trials — a paralysed woman drove a robotic arm from her motor cortex in 2012; tactile feedback was written into a participant's cortex in 2021 <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --> — while teleoperation without the nerve link is routine: a New York surgeon removed a gallbladder in Strasbourg in 2001 at a delay of about 155 milliseconds; his lunar second-each-way would still disqualify a surgeon. <!-- CHECK: lindbergh-2001 --> His reversible freezing has not been accomplished for any mammal — vitrified rat kidneys were rewarmed and transplanted with lasting function in 2023; there is no dated path from an organ to an organism, and no human has been revived — while cryonics has been selling the promise since 1972. <!-- CHECK: kidney-vitrification-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: alcor-1972 --> His telegraph paradox has the structure of the cases Parfit made canonical in <em>Reasons and Persons</em> (1984) — the teletransporter, the branch line — and the upload debate runs on them still. <!-- CHECK: parfit-1984 --> His "only probable, never certain" (p. 207) is where the skeptical literature still sits; Bostrom's simulation argument (2003) is a cousin, not the same result. <!-- CHECK: bostrom-2003 --> <!-- CHECK: chalmers-2022 --></p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-42-</section>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-43-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-44-<h2>The ledger</h2>
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-51-<tr><td>Phantomatics proper: bidirectional, "no 'exits'", indistinguishable (pp. 200–201, 207)</td><td>No shipped system meets the definition; varifocal prototypes (2018–) address one input-side problem; no dated path on the efferent, no-exits side <!-- CHECK: varifocal-prototypes --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-52-<tr><td>Later Lem, as Zylinska summarises the afterwords: VR devices "such as Eye Phone and Data Glove" among what he "did manage to predict correctly" (p. 21)</td><td>Head-worn tracked displays did arrive (row 1); on the full 1964 definition they are not phantomats (row 3); the afterwords' own wording is not in this edition</td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-53-<tr><td>"It is incomparably more difficult to construct the second one [human-producing] than the first [travel]" (p. 206)</td><td>Neither phantomat has been built, so the ranking is untested; text personas pass a five-minute test, but that is not the machine ranked <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:54:<tr><td>Phantomatics "can mainly be a form of entertainment" (p. 214) — yet "its entertainment role may turn out to be rather marginal" (p. 215)</td><td>Hedged both ways about an unbuilt machine; the headsets that shipped are games-led, but they are not it <!-- CHECK: vr-use-mix --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-55-<tr><td>"a true blessing for the blind" whose "primary visual cortex" is intact (p. 215)</td><td>Cortical visual prosthesis in a six-participant early-feasibility trial <!-- CHECK: orion-cortical --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-56-<tr><td>Teletaxy: a "remote-I" whose receptors and motor nerves are wired to yours (p. 222)</td><td>Motor read-out drives a robotic arm (2012); tactile feedback written into cortex (2021); both in trials, neither combined into a proxy body <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-early">EARLY</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-57-<tr><td>Cerebromatics: "impossible to add … absent traits" through such maneuvers (p. 216); a direct skill write "not simple even on a theoretical level" (p. 217)</td><td>Neural write in humans is stimulation and modulation; the impossibility is untested, not confirmed <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --></td><td class="verdict v-open">OPEN</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:58:<tr><td>"We can expect that such a freezing technology, promising … 100 percent chance of a future revival … will be accomplished" (p. 231)</td><td>Organ-scale vitrification in rats, 2023; no whole mammal revived and no dated path from organ to organism <!-- CHECK: kidney-vitrification-2023 --> <!-- CHECK: alcor-1972 --></td><td class="verdict v-miss">MISS</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-59-<tr><td colspan="3"><strong>Problem level</strong></td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-60-<tr><td>The cost of a fake scales with the complexity of the thing faked — Miss World cheaper than Einstein, interlocutor dearer than saucer (pp. 203, 206)</td><td>This essay's transposition: the fakes that fool people in 2026 are the cheapest ones — a warned judge loses a five-minute text test; the scarce resource was the receiver's credulity, not the simulator's complexity <!-- CHECK: jones-bergen-2025 --> <!-- CHECK: eliza-1966 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-61-<tr><td>Indistinguishability belongs to feedback systems; one-way transfer is art (p. 200)</td><td>The mass "can you tell?" problem arrived in one-way generated images — the 2023 Pentagon fake <!-- CHECK: pentagon-fake-2023 --></td><td class="verdict v-rq">RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER</td></tr>
--
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-70-</section>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-71-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-72-<h2>Where he was worst</h2>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:73:<p>He priced the wrong thing — for the problem as it actually arrived. He measured a fake by the complexity of the thing faked; the fake that fools people at scale in 2026 is a few sentences of text, because the receiver completes other minds on thin evidence, and the fake that does not exist is the one he thought cheap, the world. That is a verdict on his cost model, not on his ranking of two unbuilt machines. The narrower omission is that the chapter never proposes growing a general interlocutor by training on a text corpus at scale — language, in it, happens inside the vision; it is never what a machine is trained on. He also excluded the one-way image from the problem by classing it as art; deepfakes are the one-way image posing exactly his question. On the second half he was worse in a smaller way: reversible freezing "will be accomplished" is the chapter's cleanest MISS.</p>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-74-
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-75-<section class="open">
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-76-<h2>Still open</h2>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-77-<ul>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html:78:<li><strong>Whether the travel phantomat arrives at all.</strong> What would close it: a system that is bidirectional across the sensorium and defeats adversarial reality tests — the squats, the drawer — not a comfortable headset with a market; Meta's losses are the current price of the experiment. <!-- CHECK: reality-labs-losses --></li>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-79-<li><strong>Whether the cheap interlocutor meets Lem's own bar</strong> — an autonomy that survives a long game with a clever opponent (pp. 206–207); five minutes is not that. What would close it: a model a domain expert cannot distinguish from a peer over weeks — or a demonstrated ceiling.</li>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-80-<li><strong>Whether central phantomatics gets built.</strong> Reward-centre stimulation exists as therapy, not as his "technology of facilitated ecstasy" (p. 210). <!-- CHECK: bci-write-2026 --> What would close it: a stimulation device sold or sought for pleasure — or a record of it tried and stopped.</li>
chapters/06-the-cheap-channel.html-81-</ul>

