+
+### 9. Lem p. 351 is transposed into a position it does not state
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 7 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The Conclusion answers the objection that Lem is inventing an *Ars Magna* without adequate scientific warrant. His reply is that chromosomal predictors and brains already exist in nature and therefore imitation is a reasoned hypothesis rather than pure fantasy. It does not say his purpose is mapping conceptual limits rather than predicting. Calling it “the same position” as Swirski's report is another transposition.
+
+The p. 351 quotation is also absent from essay 9's `checks/quotes.tsv` rows, so the passing gate never checks it for this essay.
+
+### 10. The source gates have material coverage holes
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 17 — **CONFIRMED**; Grok 13's source table — **CONFIRMED**, with the qualifications below.
+
+Only two Lem quotations are registered for essay 9, both from p. 367. The p. 351 quotation and p. 43 “only narrowly specialized devices” are unregistered. The secondary gate contains 17 rows, but it omits quoted material used in the opening, including:
+
+- the polo-library clause;
+- “futurology in general and science fiction in particular…”;
+- “just in case they do not…”;
+- the artificial-emotion/VR/search-engine/nanobot/bionics/SETI list.
+
+The polo facts are also flattened: Swirski says “some fifty” mounts at prices “well in excess of half a million dollars”; the essay states exactly fifty at half a million each. More importantly, “nobody forecast them” is Swirski's universal absence claim, not a demonstrated result of a systematic corpus search. Attribute it as his example rather than repeat it as fact.
+
+The punctuated-equilibrium “eight years” claim and the “same year” Science Citation Index claim require edition control. The corpus is the revised 1974 text. The project does not currently establish that the relevant sentences and note 8 were already in the 1964 edition. The claims may be true historically, but this essay cannot assert them from the held corpus.
+
+Required revision: after prose changes, regenerate all essay-9 Lem and secondary rows from the HTML and inspect the unmatched quotations by eye. Add semantically appropriate claim rows for factual assertions; the current `swirski-2022` row does not receipt horse prices.
+
+### 11. The air-cushion paraphrase strips the governing hedge
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 3 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+Page 36 begins “One can also imagine” and later says “When the current automobile is eventually replaced…” and “it is quite likely that, say…”. Essay 9 reduces this to “He said the car would be replaced.” Essay 2 at least preserves the governing language in its ledger. If this remains an exhibit, quote enough to show the modal status; preferably use the stronger p. 231 freezing forecast or p. 367 method claim.
+
+This does not independently settle whether essay 2's MISS is fair, but it makes the unqualified paraphrase in essay 9 unfair.
+
+### 12. The Butko paragraph contains one real error and one reviewer overreach
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 12a (“knew … the man,” duplicate introduction) — **CONFIRMED**. Grok 12b (“before any English translation existed” is false) — **REJECTED**.
+
+Nothing in Butko's chapter establishes that he personally knew Lem, so “Two people who knew the book and the man” is unsupported. Butko is then introduced again in the next paragraph almost from scratch; consolidate the two paragraphs.
+
+The review overreaches about translation. In context the essay means an English translation of *Summa*, and Butko himself says the book was “unavailable in English translation” while identifying two English-language “holes” that discuss it. *A Stanislaw Lem Reader* includes “Thirty Years Later” and substantial discussion, not a translation of *Summa*. “Before the complete English translation” would remove the ambiguity, but the existing claim is not factually refuted by Butko.
+
+### 13. Calling Swirski “the man who translated Lem” is accurate, though easy to misread
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 2 — **REJECTED**.
+
+The 2006 volume locally held here prints Lem's “Smart Robots” as “Translated by Peter Swirski,” and Swirski edited and translated material in *A Stanislaw Lem Reader*. The essay says he translated Lem, not that he translated this edition of *Summa*. The claim-row phrase “one of Lem's translators” is therefore supportable.
+
+For clarity, “scholar, interviewer, editor, and translator of other Lem texts” would avoid suggesting that he is Joanna Zylinska. This is a presentation risk, not the factual error the reviewer alleges.
+
+### 14. The four MISSes are incompletely and misleadingly presented
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 15 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The four object-level MISSes in the current census are air-cushion succession, narrow specialisation, whole-organism reversible freezing, and the frequency-analysis librarian. Essay 9 names only the first two as its falsifiability proof, then presents the librarian as a separate later collision. One of the two named examples—the specialisation row—is invalid (finding 5), and the other is heavily hedged (finding 11).
+
+Name all four once, use the strongest exact language, and recompute the census after correcting essay 2. Until then, the claim that four rows were “falsified outright” is not stable.
+
+### 15. The chapter's “nobody translated” statement is false
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — CONFIRMED.**
+
+Still open #2 calls chapter 9 “the chapter nobody translated.” CONTEXT §2 explicitly records a Russian *Summa* translation and says the missing chapter can be closed with Polish **or Russian** text. The problem is that this repository's 2013 English edition omits the chapter, not that nobody translated it.
+
+Required revision: “the chapter absent from this English edition” or “the chapter this corpus lacks.”
+
+### 16. The claimed independence of Swirski and Butko is unsupported
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — UNVERIFIABLE.**
+
+The essay says the same verdict arrived “independently.” Butko's chapter appeared in a volume edited by Swirski, cites Swirski's 1997 Reader, and directs readers to it for “Thirty Years Later.” The later Swirski article could also have been informed by the 2006 chapter he edited. Their agreement is real; independence is not established by either held source.
+
+Required revision: remove “independently” unless correspondence or another source establishes intellectual independence.
+
+### 17. The lede's expertise superlative is unsupported
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — UNVERIFIABLE.**
+
+Swirski plainly has deep expertise, but “knows the material better than anyone writing here” is a comparative claim the essay neither defines nor receipts. It adds authority without evidence and is unnecessary to present the strongest case.
+
+Required revision: replace with concrete credentials: scholar, interviewer, editor, and translator of other Lem texts.
+
+### 18. The essay is not missing a mandatory negative in the way Agy claims
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Agy 6 — **REJECTED**.
+
+AGENT rule 2 requires at least one MISS **or** RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER, and the essay contains RQWA. The user's review prompt did not ask for “what Lem got wrong in the missing chapter.” Agy also says the essay “includes a MISS” on ariadnology, but the actual verdict is RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON. The useful part of this reviewer's instinct is already captured in finding 7: the essay should grade the critics' own generous scorecards, not manufacture a negative on absent chapter 9.
