@@ -0,0 +1,211 @@
+**Verdict: BLOCK — revise before shipping; the essay misattributes its central sentence, misstates Swirski's argument, violates the ledger contract, and relies on two already-unsound object-level examples.**
+
+# Essay 9 consolidated review
+
+I checked both reports against `CONTEXT.md`, `AGENT.md`, the essay, the existing ledgers and claim registers, the locally held Swirski and Butko texts, and the Lem corpus with `scripts/find.py`. `./verify.sh` passes, but that does not clear the chapter: both quote gates validate registered rows only, and several quotations in this essay were never registered.
+
+## Ranked findings
+
+### 1. The essay turns Swirski's report into a verbatim statement by Lem
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 1 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The essay calls the sentence about “mapping the conceptual limits of science” “Lem's own” and labels the ledger row “Lem to Swirski, 1992.” Swirski p. 40 actually writes in third-person reported speech: “As Lem himself told me … more than in predicting the future **he** has always been more interested…”. The locally held source does not contain Lem's 1992 wording, and *A Stanislaw Lem Reader*, where the interviews are collected, is not held here. The census can support a claim about the pattern of this book's verdicts; it cannot establish Lem's motive or interest.
+
+Required revision: attribute the statement to Swirski's report, not as a quotation from Lem. Do not score Lem's interest as a HIT unless the interview text is obtained.
+
+### 2. The closing argument contains a major numerical contradiction missed by both reviewers
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — CONFIRMED.**
+
+“The deeper problem” paragraph says problem-level claims resist scoring, “which is why thirty-seven rows stand OPEN,” and concludes that “the part of him that holds up is the part that cannot be checked.” The registered census says otherwise: of 37 OPEN rows, **23 are object-level and 14 are problem-level**. Twenty-one of the 35 problem rows have a non-OPEN verdict (15 HIT, 6 RQWA). The paragraph assigns the whole book's OPEN count to one layer and then calls 43% HIT “cannot be checked.”
+
+Required revision: use the actual layer counts. More fundamentally, do not infer reliability from “no problem-level MISSes” without acknowledging that the verdict scheme routes a live dilemma with a wrong resolution to RQWA rather than MISS. Part of the apparent asymmetry is built into the categories.
+
+### 3. “Heads I win, tails you lose” is turned into a charge Swirski did not make about *Summa*
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 4 — **CONFIRMED**. Agy 3 — **REJECTED** as framed.
+
+Swirski's phrase closes his deliberately absurd forecast for the year 1,002,022 (pp. 36–37 of the article). He then turns to *Summa*, calls it futurological speculation, and lists specific anticipations he thinks landed. The essay rewrites the parody as “Lem's forecasts are unfalsifiable by construction” and announces that half of Swirski's case has failed. That is a straw man.
+
+Agy is right that the present RQWA row is incoherent, but wrong that the correction is “simply a MISS.” The charge is not a forecast or event to which §4's MISS definition applies, and it was not Swirski's claim about *Summa* in the first place. Remove the row or restate the claim Swirski actually makes: scientific futurology would require unequivocal concrete predictions and reasonable timelines, while Lem's hits are byproducts of a different intellectual project.
+
+### 4. The ledger violates the authority document and misuses the two layers
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 8 — **CONFIRMED**; Agy 1 — **CONFIRMED**; Agy 5 and Grok 18 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+CONTEXT §4 defines a row as one specific, paged Lem claim against one specific receipted fact, with object and problem layers kept separate. This ledger instead grades claims by Swirski and Butko, then places literary-critical propositions such as “not a futurologist,” cherry-picking, edition history, motive, and category error beneath “Object level” and “Problem level.” None is an object-level device, date, or quantity. Rows 1 and 5 use the same 22%/43% census to pay two different HITs.
+
+The amended essay-9 contract authorizes testing the scholarly case against the ledgers; it does not explicitly suspend §4. Either amend the authority document again and use meta-ledger headings with a defined unit of judgment, or keep the critics in prose and show a compact, representative subset of the actual Lem rows. As written, the two-layer headings are decoration and the skeptical reader is promised data but shown a scorecard of critics.
+
+Also cut “no detractor would have predicted” the result. Swirski, Butko, and Zylinska all argue substantially that Lem's questions/conceptual problems matter more than his determinate forecasts.
+
+### 5. The specialisation example is not a failed forecast, and GPT-3 cannot receipt the claimed macro-reversal
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 10 — **CONFIRMED**; Agy 2 — **CONFIRMED**; Agy 4 — **CONFIRMED**; Grok 13 (`gpt3-2020`) — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+On p. 43 Lem writes that technical evolution “**has so far been** moving” toward narrowly specialised devices. This is a description of the record up to the text's date. The next page explicitly locates future universality in adaptively self-programming systems. Essay 9 drops “has so far been,” calls the premise a falsified forecast, and uses GPT-3 to prove that “the direction reversed.” A single general-purpose model does not establish the direction of technical evolution as a whole, while specialised chips, sensors, medical devices, and software remain ubiquitous.
+
+This finding also exposes a defect in essay 2's current MISS row, which essay 9 imported without rechecking. It cannot be used as one of the book's four clean falsifications until essay 2 is corrected and the census recomputed.
+
+### 6. The ariadnology argument conflates three different claims
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 5 — **CONFIRMED**; Grok 11 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+Swirski's “cherry-picking his fiction” sentence refers to *The Magellan Nebula*, not to the separate paragraph reporting Lem's later self-grades of *Summa*. The ledger splices the two and assigns RAWR, a verdict that does not fit an accusation of cherry-picking.
+
+The essay then overstates note 8. Lem is demonstrably wrong that frequency analysis and algorithmic selection are worthless; citation indexing and PageRank refute that method claim. But the machine he specifies is an addressing librarian “sending the right kinds of information to the right kinds of people.” Essay 7 itself leaves that full specification open: citation counting helps find central work but does not perform Lem's complete addressing task. “He rules out precisely the machine that worked” and “machines lacking comprehension provide it” collapse the method, the need, and the complete machine.
+
+Required revision: separate (a) Swirski's fiction charge, unverified here; (b) later Lem's self-credit for ariadnology; (c) the p. 367 MISS on frequency analysis; and (d) the still-OPEN addressing librarian.
+
+### 7. The critics' own scorecards are not tested, despite being the essay's subject
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 6 — **CONFIRMED** (with the narrow qualification below); Grok 14 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+“The two scorecards collide once” is defensible only if “two” means later Lem's self-grade versus this book and only locally checkable self-credit is counted. It is not adequate for the essay's stated job, which is to test Swirski and Butko. Swirski tentatively credits virtual reality and “the failure of Frank Drake's SETI”; this book scores phantomatics proper OPEN, later Lem's EyePhone/DataGlove self-grade OPEN, and the SETI silence OPEN. Butko explicitly calls ariadnology fulfilled on p. 99, so the held Butko text supplies another critic's collision with essay 7.
+
+The essay needs a genuine negative on the scholarly case: run the critics' credited examples through essays 3, 6, and 7. Do not force a MISS merely to satisfy AGENT rule 2; a properly supported RQWA is sufficient.
+
+### 8. The census is asked to prove propositions it does not measure
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 9 — **CONFIRMED**; Grok 13 (“specificity”) — **CONFIRMED**; Grok 16 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+A 22% object HIT rate versus 43% problem HIT rate supports the narrow descriptive result that these ledgers grade Lem better on problems than objects. It does not by itself establish Swirski's normative conclusion that Lem “should not be read as a futurologist,” Lem's subjective interest, or the causal claim that failures cluster “where he was most specific.” Specific object claims also produced eleven HITs.
+
+The “provisional signposts” row is too metaphorical to satisfy §4 and is scored HIT even though its own evidence cell says the metaphor conceals the result. Cut it or restate a specific, testable claim. Likewise, “four MISSes, eleven half-failures, thirty-seven questions” is not “this book's totals”: it silently omits 26 HIT, 4 EARLY, and 3 RAWR rows.
+
+### 9. Lem p. 351 is transposed into a position it does not state
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 7 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The Conclusion answers the objection that Lem is inventing an *Ars Magna* without adequate scientific warrant. His reply is that chromosomal predictors and brains already exist in nature and therefore imitation is a reasoned hypothesis rather than pure fantasy. It does not say his purpose is mapping conceptual limits rather than predicting. Calling it “the same position” as Swirski's report is another transposition.
+
+The p. 351 quotation is also absent from essay 9's `checks/quotes.tsv` rows, so the passing gate never checks it for this essay.
+
+### 10. The source gates have material coverage holes
+
+**Reviewer findings:** Grok 17 — **CONFIRMED**; Grok 13's source table — **CONFIRMED**, with the qualifications below.
+
+Only two Lem quotations are registered for essay 9, both from p. 367. The p. 351 quotation and p. 43 “only narrowly specialized devices” are unregistered. The secondary gate contains 17 rows, but it omits quoted material used in the opening, including:
+
+- the polo-library clause;
+- “futurology in general and science fiction in particular…”;
+- “just in case they do not…”;
+- the artificial-emotion/VR/search-engine/nanobot/bionics/SETI list.
+
+The polo facts are also flattened: Swirski says “some fifty” mounts at prices “well in excess of half a million dollars”; the essay states exactly fifty at half a million each. More importantly, “nobody forecast them” is Swirski's universal absence claim, not a demonstrated result of a systematic corpus search. Attribute it as his example rather than repeat it as fact.
+
+The punctuated-equilibrium “eight years” claim and the “same year” Science Citation Index claim require edition control. The corpus is the revised 1974 text. The project does not currently establish that the relevant sentences and note 8 were already in the 1964 edition. The claims may be true historically, but this essay cannot assert them from the held corpus.
+
+Required revision: after prose changes, regenerate all essay-9 Lem and secondary rows from the HTML and inspect the unmatched quotations by eye. Add semantically appropriate claim rows for factual assertions; the current `swirski-2022` row does not receipt horse prices.
+
+### 11. The air-cushion paraphrase strips the governing hedge
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 3 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+Page 36 begins “One can also imagine” and later says “When the current automobile is eventually replaced…” and “it is quite likely that, say…”. Essay 9 reduces this to “He said the car would be replaced.” Essay 2 at least preserves the governing language in its ledger. If this remains an exhibit, quote enough to show the modal status; preferably use the stronger p. 231 freezing forecast or p. 367 method claim.
+
+This does not independently settle whether essay 2's MISS is fair, but it makes the unqualified paraphrase in essay 9 unfair.
+
+### 12. The Butko paragraph contains one real error and one reviewer overreach
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 12a (“knew … the man,” duplicate introduction) — **CONFIRMED**. Grok 12b (“before any English translation existed” is false) — **REJECTED**.
+
+Nothing in Butko's chapter establishes that he personally knew Lem, so “Two people who knew the book and the man” is unsupported. Butko is then introduced again in the next paragraph almost from scratch; consolidate the two paragraphs.
+
+The review overreaches about translation. In context the essay means an English translation of *Summa*, and Butko himself says the book was “unavailable in English translation” while identifying two English-language “holes” that discuss it. *A Stanislaw Lem Reader* includes “Thirty Years Later” and substantial discussion, not a translation of *Summa*. “Before the complete English translation” would remove the ambiguity, but the existing claim is not factually refuted by Butko.
+
+### 13. Calling Swirski “the man who translated Lem” is accurate, though easy to misread
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 2 — **REJECTED**.
+
+The 2006 volume locally held here prints Lem's “Smart Robots” as “Translated by Peter Swirski,” and Swirski edited and translated material in *A Stanislaw Lem Reader*. The essay says he translated Lem, not that he translated this edition of *Summa*. The claim-row phrase “one of Lem's translators” is therefore supportable.
+
+For clarity, “scholar, interviewer, editor, and translator of other Lem texts” would avoid suggesting that he is Joanna Zylinska. This is a presentation risk, not the factual error the reviewer alleges.
+
+### 14. The four MISSes are incompletely and misleadingly presented
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Grok 15 — **CONFIRMED**.
+
+The four object-level MISSes in the current census are air-cushion succession, narrow specialisation, whole-organism reversible freezing, and the frequency-analysis librarian. Essay 9 names only the first two as its falsifiability proof, then presents the librarian as a separate later collision. One of the two named examples—the specialisation row—is invalid (finding 5), and the other is heavily hedged (finding 11).
+
+Name all four once, use the strongest exact language, and recompute the census after correcting essay 2. Until then, the claim that four rows were “falsified outright” is not stable.
+
+### 15. The chapter's “nobody translated” statement is false
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — CONFIRMED.**
+
+Still open #2 calls chapter 9 “the chapter nobody translated.” CONTEXT §2 explicitly records a Russian *Summa* translation and says the missing chapter can be closed with Polish **or Russian** text. The problem is that this repository's 2013 English edition omits the chapter, not that nobody translated it.
+
+Required revision: “the chapter absent from this English edition” or “the chapter this corpus lacks.”
+
+### 16. The claimed independence of Swirski and Butko is unsupported
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — UNVERIFIABLE.**
+
+The essay says the same verdict arrived “independently.” Butko's chapter appeared in a volume edited by Swirski, cites Swirski's 1997 Reader, and directs readers to it for “Thirty Years Later.” The later Swirski article could also have been informed by the 2006 chapter he edited. Their agreement is real; independence is not established by either held source.
+
+Required revision: remove “independently” unless correspondence or another source establishes intellectual independence.
+
+### 17. The lede's expertise superlative is unsupported
+
+**MISSED BY BOTH — UNVERIFIABLE.**
+
+Swirski plainly has deep expertise, but “knows the material better than anyone writing here” is a comparative claim the essay neither defines nor receipts. It adds authority without evidence and is unnecessary to present the strongest case.
+
+Required revision: replace with concrete credentials: scholar, interviewer, editor, and translator of other Lem texts.
+
+### 18. The essay is not missing a mandatory negative in the way Agy claims
+
+**Reviewer finding:** Agy 6 — **REJECTED**.
+
+AGENT rule 2 requires at least one MISS **or** RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER, and the essay contains RQWA. The user's review prompt did not ask for “what Lem got wrong in the missing chapter.” Agy also says the essay “includes a MISS” on ariadnology, but the actual verdict is RIGHT ANSWER, WRONG REASON. The useful part of this reviewer's instinct is already captured in finding 7: the essay should grade the critics' own generous scorecards, not manufacture a negative on absent chapter 9.
+
+## Complete reviewer disposition
+
+This index makes the adjudication of every submitted finding explicit.
+
+| Reviewer item | Status | Consolidated finding |
+|---|---|---|
+| Grok 1 | CONFIRMED | 1 |
+| Grok 2 | REJECTED | 13 |
